# DianaSh golden corpus. Recorded from the C# shell, byte for byte; EMUSEN_DIANASH_GOLDEN=record rewrites it - see EmuSen_DianaSh.md §13.4.
# Patterns: <ROOT> is the sandbox's real root, <TEMP> the corpus's real temporary folder, <INSTALL> the install's real directory;
# a 'match' block is a regular expression the whole output must match, written where a line prints a time or a measured number.
command man
usage 1   man [command]                 with no argument, same as 'help'; with a command name, its full manual page

case with no argument it is help
target none
covers 1
$ man
match 26388
^Available commands:\n  help                          this text - every command, one line each, with a brief explanation\n  man \[command\]                 with no argument, same as 'help'; with a command name, its full manual page\n  spaces                        list available memory spaces\n  mem <space> <addr> \[<len>\]    hexdump <len> bytes \(default 16\) from <space> at <addr>\n  write <space> <addr> <value>  write one byte \(only if <space> is writable\)\n  regs                          CPU \+ video \(\+ APU/coprocessor\) registers\n  regs <cpu>                    just one processor's registers - `cpus` lists them\n  \(to WRITE a register, see `setreg` - kept separate so `regs` stays read-only\)\n  cophist \[<reg>\] \[<count>\]     Coprocessor register history \(default: all regs, last 16\)\n  sprites                       active sprite/OBJ table\n  pal \[<index>\]                 one palette, or all of them if omitted\n  channels                      list audio channels/voices - index, active, envelope level \(0-100\), muted, core-specific detail \(see `mute` to isolate one\)\n  mute <index> <on\|off>         mute/unmute one audio channel - channel's own playback state still advances, it's just excluded from the mix\n  watch add <space> <addr> <len> \[write\|read\|both\]\n                                register a watch, prints matching accesses live \+ stores them\n                                \(default write, matching this command's original behavior\)\n  watch list                    list active watches with their IDs and access kind\n  watch log <id> \[<count>\]      show a watch's recorded events \(default 20\)\n  watch summary <id>            group a watch's recorded events by access site \(Context\),\n                                showing each unique site once with a hit count instead of\n                                every individual event - the dynamic, addressing-mode-agnostic\n                                equivalent of `readers`/`writers`, which only find statically-\n                                visible absolute/absolute-long instructions and miss indexed/\n                                indirect access entirely\. Only covers whatever's still in the\n                                watch's own bounded event buffer, same as `watch log`\.\n  watch clear <id>              clear a watch's stored events \(doesn't remove the watch\)\n  watch remove <id>             remove a watch entirely\n  bp add <addr>\[-<end>\] \[if <expr>\] \[log <expr>\]\n                                halt just before <addr> runs \(24-bit CPU address, or a range\);\n                                with 'log' it records <expr> and carries on instead of halting\n  bp write <space> <addr>\[-<end>\] \[<v>\] \[changed\] \[if <expr>\]\n                                halt just after anything writes that address\n  bp read <space> <addr>\[-<end>\] \[<v>\] \[if <expr>\]\n                                halt just after anything reads it\n  bp uninit <space>             halt the first time never-written memory is read back\n  bp when <condition> \[log\|off\] halt \(or just record\) when the hardware does something\n                                it should not - `bp when` alone lists what this core detects\n  bp depth <n>                  halt if the call stack ever gets deeper than <n>\n  bp forbid <addr>-<end>        never halt while the PC is in this range\n  bp log \[<count>\] \| bp log clear\n                                show \(or forget\) what the logpoints have recorded\n  bp list                       list active breakpoints with their IDs and hit counts\n  bp on\|off <id>                enable/disable one breakpoint without removing it\n  bp remove <id>                remove a breakpoint entirely\n  bp <cpu> add\|list\|remove \.\.\.  same, on another processor - `cpus` lists them, and each\n                                one's addresses are its own address space\n  \(see also: `cpus`, `step`, `runto`, and the F4 prompt's own 'continue'/'c'\)\n  runto nmi \| irq \| brk \| cop   resume until the next interrupt of that kind is taken\n  runto scanline <n>            resume until scanline <n> starts\n  runto frame \[<n>\]             resume until frame <n> \(default: the next one\)\n  runto <addr>                  resume until <addr> executes \(a one-shot breakpoint\)\n  bt \[<count>\]                  backtrace: the live call chain, innermost frame first\n  bt reset                      forget the current chain \(after a stack-manipulating routine\)\n  bt <cpu> \[<count>\|reset\]      same, on another processor - `cpus` lists them\n  eval <expr>                   evaluate an expression against live state \(decimal, hex, binary\)\n  eval symbols \[<filter>\]       list every symbol an expression can name\n  eval <cpu> <expr>             evaluate against another processor's registers - `cpus` lists them\n  label add <addr> <name> \[<comment\.\.\.>\]   name an address\n  label list \[<filter>\]                    list labels, address order\n  label at <addr>                          what is named at \(or nearest below\) <addr>\n  label remove <name\|addr>                 drop one label\n  label clear                              drop every label\n  label load <path>                        merge a label file in\n  label save <path>                        write every label out\n  counters on <space>           start tallying reads/writes/executes for <space>\n  counters off                  stop tallying \(kept counts stay readable\)\n  counters clear                zero every count, stay armed\n  counters <addr> \[<len>\]       totals over a range, default length 1\n  counters top \[r\|w\|x\|u\] \[<n>\]  hottest addresses, default reads, default 20\n  counters cold <addr> <len>    addresses in the range nothing ever touched\n  freeze add <space> <addr> \[<value>\]  pin <addr>; without <value>, at whatever it holds now\n  freeze list                          list frozen addresses and how many writes each blocked\n  freeze remove <id>                   unfreeze one address\n  freeze clear                         unfreeze everything\n  profile on                    start charging instructions to the innermost routine\n  profile off                   stop \(collected counts stay readable\)\n  profile clear                 zero the counts, stay armed\n  profile top \[<n>\]             hottest routines, default 20\n  profile <cpu> on\|off\|top \.\.\.  same, on another processor - `cpus` lists them\n  cpus                          list the processors a debug command can be scoped to\n  copflow on \[<size>\]           start logging coprocessor register-window traffic\n  copflow off                   stop logging\n  copflow clear                 forget everything logged so far\n  copflow tail \[<n>\]            the last <n> accesses in order \(default 20\)\n  copflow stats                 per-register read/write tallies, busiest first\n  copflow poll                  the longest run of unchanging reads - a stuck handshake\n  cov on\|off                    start/stop recording executed addresses\n  cov clear                     forget everything recorded so far\n  cov <addr> \[<len>\]            report which of <len> bytes from <addr> ever executed\n                                \(default 16\); 0 executed means the code never ran\n  cov mark                      remember what has run so far, so `cov new` can subtract it\n  cov new <addr> \[<len>\]        only what has run SINCE the mark - do the thing, then ask\n  cov funcs \[<count>\]           routines discovered by watching calls land, busiest first\n  cov save\|load <file>          persist the map to Logs/<CoreName>/<file> and merge it back\n  cov <cpu> on\|off\|clear\|<addr> same, on another processor's own instruction stream -\n                                `cpus` lists them, and each has its own address space\n  setreg <reg> <value>          write one of the main CPU's registers\n  setreg <cpu> <reg> <value>    same, on another processor - `cpus` lists them\n  vectors                       where each interrupt vector points, resolved through labels\n  addr <addr>                   what a CPU-bus address decodes to \(ROM offset, RAM, a register\)\n  dma                           every channel's current configuration\n  dma log on \[<n>\] \| dma log off\n                                start/stop recording transfers \(<n> retained, default 4096\)\n  dma log \[<n>\] \| dma log clear\n                                the last <n> transfers in order \(default 20\), or forget them\n  dma stats                     per-channel and per-destination totals, biggest first\n  framelog add <space> <addr> \[<width>\]\n                                register a per-frame value sample \(width 1/2/4 bytes, default 1\)\n  framelog list                 list active frame logs with their IDs\n  framelog show <id> \[<count>\]  show a frame log's recorded \(frame, value\) samples \(default 20\)\n  framelog clear <id>           clear a frame log's stored samples \(doesn't remove it\)\n  framelog remove <id>          remove a frame log entirely\n  cheat add <code> \[description\]    decode a code, guessing whether it's Pro\n                                     Action Replay/Game Wizard or Game Genie from\n                                     its formatting \(see `gg` if it guesses wrong -\n                                     both formats use the same 8 hex-digit\n                                     characters, so this is a best-effort guess,\n                                     not a guarantee\)\n  cheat poke <space> <addr> <value> \[description\]\n                                     add a raw RAM-poke cheat directly, bypassing\n                                     code decoding \(e\.g\. an address you already\n                                     found with `search`\)\n  cheat gg <code> \[description\]     decode a real SNES Game Genie code \(8\n                                     characters\) and add it as a ROM patch, enabled\n                                     - use this instead of `add` when its guess is\n                                     wrong for a code formatted unconventionally\n  cheat rompatch <addr> <value> \[<compare>\|-\] \[description\]\n                                     add a Game Genie-style ROM-read intercept\n                                     directly, bypassing code decoding: substitute\n                                     <value> for reads of cartridge address <addr>\.\n                                     <compare> gates the patch to only apply while\n                                     the real byte there equals it \(pass - to skip,\n                                     an unconditional patch - what every real SNES\n                                     Game Genie code decodes to; unlike NES/Genesis,\n                                     SNES Game Genie codes never carry a compare\)\n  cheat list                         list every cheat with its ID, kind, and state\n  cheat master \[on\|off\]              one switch over every cheat at once, without\n                                     touching any cheat's own enabled state \(no\n                                     argument reports which way it is set\)\n  cheat enable <id>                  turn a cheat back on\n  cheat disable <id>                 turn a cheat off without removing it\n  cheat remove <id>                  remove a cheat entirely\n  cheat clear                        remove every cheat\n  cheat save <name>                  write every cheat to\n                                     /etc/EmuSen/cheats/<name>\.json\n  cheat load <name>                  add every cheat from that file to whatever\n                                     is already loaded \(`cheat clear` first to\n                                     replace rather than merge\)\n  cheat files                        list the saved cheat files\n  cheat import <path\.cht>            import RetroArch/libretro \.cht cheats, added\n                                     disabled so nothing turns on by surprise\n  cheat export <path\.cht>            write RAM-poke cheats out as a \.cht file\n  cheat db \[status\]                  where the cheat database is, and what is in it\n  cheat db find <game>               search the database for a game\n  cheat db load <game>               import that game's cheats, disabled\n  cheat db update                    download the libretro cheat database \(CC BY-SA 4\.0\)\n  cheat db prune \[--apply\]           delete the systems no core in this build can use\n  memcard ls \[<card>\]           every card under Saves, or the saves on one card\n  memcard cp <card>:<save> <card>\n                                copy a save onto another card of its medium\n  memcard mv <card>:<save> <card>\n                                copy a save, read it back, then delete the first\n  memcard rm <card>:<save>      delete a save as the console's own manager does\n  memcard import <card> <from>\.\.\.\n                                every save of <from> onto <card>, all or none\n  memcard new <card>            a new card, formatted where its medium allows\n  memcard dup <card> <new>      a whole card copied to a new name\n  memcard rename <card> <new>   a card renamed\n  search <space> <val> \[<w>\]    start a new search: find every address currently equal to <val>\n                                \(width <w> in bytes: 1, 2, or 4 - default 1, little-endian\)\n  search refine <val>           narrow the current search to addresses now equal to <val>\n  search changed\|unchanged      narrow to addresses whose value did/didn't change since last search/refine\n  search increased\|decreased    narrow to addresses whose value went up/down since last search/refine\n  search list \[<count>\]         list current candidate addresses \+ values \(default 20\)\n  search reset                  clear the current search\n  memfind <space> <bytes> \[<max>\]  find every offset where <bytes> occurs \(hex pairs, '\?\?' matches\n                                any byte, separators optional\) - default at most 20 hits\n  snapshot <space> <name>       capture <space>'s full current contents under <name>\n  snapshot list                 list saved snapshots \(name, space, size\)\n  snapshot remove <name>        delete a saved snapshot\n  diff <name> \[<count>\]         compare snapshot <name> against that space's CURRENT contents,\n                                print addresses that changed \(default 20 shown\)\n  dump <space> <addr> <len> <file> write raw bytes to home/Logs/<CoreName>/<file>\n  load <space> <addr> <file>    write home/Logs/<CoreName>/<file>'s raw bytes into <space> starting at <addr>\n  tile <space> <addr> <bpp>     ASCII-decode one 8x8 tile \(bpp meaning is core-specific - 2/4/8 on SNES\)\n  tilemap <space> <addr> <cols> <rows>\n                                decode <cols>x<rows> tilemap entries starting at <addr>\n                                as a text grid, one already-formatted label per cell -\n                                see IDebugTarget\.DecodeTilemapEntry for what a label\n                                means on the current core\. Caller supplies the exact base\n                                address \(see BG1SC/etc via `regs`\) - this doesn't resolve\n                                BG-layer-to-tilemap-address mapping itself\.\n  disasm <space> <addr> \[<n>\]   disassemble <n> instructions \(default 10\)\n  disasm <cpu> \[<addr>\] \[<n>\]   same, in that processor's own code space and ISA -\n                                with no <addr>, starts at where it is executing now\n  \.\.\. \[m8\|m16\] \[x8\|x16\]         65816 only: force the starting operand widths\n  trace <count>                 arm a live CPU instruction trace for the next <count> instructions\n  trace off                     cancel an in-progress trace early\n  callers <addr> \[<scanstart> <scanlen>\]\n                                find instructions statically calling/jumping to <addr>,\n                                scanning CpuBus \(default: <addr>'s own bank, \$8000-\$FFFF\)\n  writers <addr> \[<scanstart> <scanlen>\]\n                                find instructions statically writing to <addr>,\n                                scanning CpuBus \(default: <addr>'s own bank, \$8000-\$FFFF\)\n  readers <addr> \[<scanstart> <scanlen>\]\n                                find instructions statically reading <addr>,\n                                scanning CpuBus \(default: <addr>'s own bank, \$8000-\$FFFF\)\n  log off                       silence every logging flag at once, without changing them\n  log on                        restore whatever each logging flag was individually set to\n  log status                    show whether the master switch is currently on or off\n  echo <text\.\.\.>                print <text> back, joined by spaces\n  sed s/pat/repl/\[gi\]           substitute \(first match per line, or every match with 'g';\n                                'i' = case-insensitive\)\. Standalone: sed <expr> <text\.\.\.>\.\n                                Real use is as a pipeline stage: <command> \| sed s/pat/repl/\n  grep \[-i\] \[-v\] \[-n\] \[-c\] \[-q\] <pattern> \[text\.\.\.\]\n                                filter lines matching <pattern> \(regex\) - -i case-insensitive,\n                                -v invert \(non-matching\), -n prefix line numbers, -c print only\n                                the match count, -q no output \(exit code only, for if/while\)\.\n                                Reads piped stdin if given, else operates on trailing literal text\.\n  wc \[-l\] \[-w\] \[-c\] \[path\]     count lines/words/bytes \(all three if no flag given\);\n                                reads piped stdin, or a real file if <path> is given\n  sort \[-n\] \[-r\]                sort input lines \(numeric with -n, reversed with -r\) -\n                                reads piped stdin, or trailing literal text\n  uniq \[-c\]                     collapse adjacent duplicate lines \(-c prefixes each with its\n                                repeat count\) - sort first for global dedup, not just adjacent\n  awk \[-F sep\] 'prog' \[path\]   run a small AWK subset over stdin or <path>, line by line\n                                patterns: /regex/, NR==N \(also !=,<,<=,>,>=\), BEGIN, END\n                                actions: \{print \$0\|\$N\|\$NF\|NR\|NF\|"lit"\[, \.\.\.\]\[; print \.\.\.\]\}\n  ls \[-a\] \[-l\] \[path\]           list directory entries \(default: current directory\);\n                                -a include dotfiles, -l show type/size/modified-time\n  cd \[dir\]                      change directory, walled to the project root \(no arg: your home directory\)\n  mv <src> <dst>                SUSPENDED - move/rename a file or directory \(dst may be an existing directory\)\n  cp \[-r\] <src> <dst>           copy a file \(-r: copy a directory and everything in it; dst may be an existing directory\)\n  rm \[-r\] <path>                SUSPENDED - delete a file \(-r: delete a directory and everything in it\)\n  pwd                           print the current working directory\n  cat <path>\.\.\.                 print one or more files' contents\n  head \[-n N\] \[path\]            first N lines \(default 10\) of stdin or a file\n  tail \[-n N\] \[path\]            last N lines \(default 10\) of stdin or a file\n  touch <path>                  create an empty file, or update an existing one's modified time\n  find \[path\] \[-name pattern\]   recursively list files/directories, optionally filtered by a glob\n  xxd \| hexdump <path>          hexdump a real file's raw bytes, or piped stdin \(UTF8-encoded first\)\n  nano <path>                   simple full-screen text editor \(Ctrl\+O save, Ctrl\+X exit - interactive terminal only\)\n  tmux new \[name\]               create a session \(auto-named if omitted\) and switch to it\n  tmux list                     list every session, \* marks the current one\n  tmux switch <name>            switch to an existing session\n  tmux kill <name>              destroy a session \(can't kill the last one\)\n  ps \| jobs                     list active breakpoints/watches/sessions as one table - see `kill`\n  kill <id>                     remove a breakpoint/watch/session by its `ps` id \(bp<N>, watch<N>, or a session name\)\n  whoami                        print the account this shell is currently running as\n  who                           list every session and which account it's running as\n  su \[name\]                     switch this shell to <name> \(default: root\) - see `useradd`\n  useradd <name>                add a new account \(root only\) - see `su`\n  userdel <name>                remove an account \(root only\) - refuses root itself or one currently in use\n  passwd <newpassword>          change your own password\n  passwd <name> <newpassword>   change <name>'s password \(root only\)\n  coretop \[-w\]                  live htop-style dashboard of the loaded core's hardware \(Ctrl\+C to exit - interactive terminal only;\n                                -w opens it in a separate window instead, if this frontend supports one\)\n  vstop \[-w\]                    live htop-style dashboard of the \.NET runtime this process runs on \(Ctrl\+C to exit - interactive terminal only;\n                                -w opens it in a separate window instead, if this frontend supports one\)\n  clear                         clear the terminal screen\n  true                          do nothing, succeed \(exit code 0\) - for while/if conditions\n  false                         do nothing, fail \(exit code 1\) - for while/if conditions\n  test EXPR / \[ EXPR \]         VALUE \(true if non-empty\) \| -z/-n VALUE \| -f/-d PATH \| !\n                                VALUE = / != / -eq / -ne / -lt / -le / -gt / -ge VALUE\n                                exit code only \(0 = true\) - meant for if/while, not for its own output\n  \[ EXPR \]                      alias for 'test EXPR' - the '\]' is required\n  history                       list previously executed commands, numbered\n  history clear                 forget all recorded history\n  !N / !!                       re-run history entry N / the last command \(typed at the prompt\)\n  resume \| continue \| c         resume emulation after an interactive halt \(F4/breakpoint/single-step\)\n  shutdown \| quit \| exit        terminate the process\n  step \| s \[<count>\]            single-step one CPU instruction \(or <count> of them\)\n  step over                     run the call at the current instruction to completion, then halt\n  step out                      run until the current routine returns to its caller\n  step <cpu> \[<count>\|over\|out\] same, stepping another processor - `cpus` lists them\n  core <corename> <path>        load or swap the running ROM \(validated against the known core registry\)\n  core <corename> --console     start that core's console with nothing in it, where it can\n  state save\|load \[path\]        save/load emulator state \(defaults to this frontend's own save-state path if omitted\)\n  hotaru \[--output window\] <rom>  play <rom> in Hotaru's game window, with its hotkeys, feed and\n                                debug windows; the shell keeps its prompt\n  summary                       free-text state dump \(whatever isn't structured above yet\)\n(?:  chd ls \[<path>\.\.\.\]            the library's disc games under each path \(relative to the library\), each with its state\n  chd convert <path>\.\.\. \[--fast\] \[-j <n>\]\n                                convert them to CHD in Compressed, each checked three ways and given a package\n  chd check \[<path>\.\.\.\]         check converted discs again: chdman's verify, every track, the table of contents\n  chd wait \[chd<n>\]             follow a chd job that went on in the background \(ps lists them, kill stops one\)\n  pkg make <path>\.\.\. \[--self-contained\] \[--zip\]\n                                a package for each game under each path \(relative to the library\), linked\n                                unless the game is to be copied in; --zip also writes the package as one zip\n  pkg ls \[<console>\]            the packages, with mode and state \(ok, moved, missing, changed\)\n  pkg check \[<package>\.\.\.\]      the game and every file hashed against the manifest \(relative to Packages\); a package's zip too\n  pkg migrate <package>\.\.\.      the game's saves, states, records, settings, art and cheats copied in\n  pkg patch add <package> <file>\n                                an IPS, UPS or BPS patch checked, copied in and applied at load\n  pkg patch rm <package> <n>    patch n taken out of the package's list\n  pkg zip <package>\.\.\.          a self-contained package written as one zip beside it, saves and all\n  pkg identity <package> original\|patched\n                                which identity the scraper sends for a patched package\n  pkg wait \[pkg<n>\]             follow a pkg job that went on in the background\n)?  export NAME\[=value\]           set a shell variable \(no real subprocess env to export TO -\n                                kept for script compatibility with real bash habits\)\n  unset NAME                    remove a shell variable\n  source <path> \(alias: \.\)      run a script file's lines in this same shell \(variables/history scope\)\n  which <name>\.\.\.               say what answers each name: an alias, a shell builtin or a command\n  command \[-v\] <name> \[args\]    run <name> as itself, not as an alias; -v prints the names that would run\n  trust <path>                  approve a module file you added, by its hash \(typed at the prompt only\)\n  if/then/elif/else/fi, for/in/do/done, while\|until/do/done, break, continue\n                                control flow - see the reference doc for the full grammar\n\nAddresses/values are hex; an optional 0x or \$ prefix is fine either way\.\nShell features: quoting \('\.\.\.'/"\.\.\."\), \$VAR/\$\{VAR\}/\$\? , \$\(command substitution\),\npipes \(\|\), redirection \(> >> <\), sequencing \(; && \|\|\), if/for/while - see\nEmuSen_Debugging_Tools_Reference_v5\.md for the full writeup and what's NOT supported\.\z
status 0
action none
$ man | tail -n 2
out 159
