case a name with no page
target none
covers 1
$ man nosuch
out 65
No manual entry for 'nosuch'. Type 'help' for a list of commands.
status 1
action none
$ echo $?
out 1
1
status 0
action none
$ man BP
out 15811
NAME
    bp - manage execution breakpoints

SYNOPSIS
    bp add <addr>[-<end>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp write <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [changed] [if <expr>]
    bp read <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [if <expr>]
    bp uninit <space>
    bp when [<condition>] [log|off]
    bp depth <n>|off
    bp forbid <addr>-<end>
    bp log [<count>] | bp log clear
    bp list
    bp on|off <id>
    bp remove <id>
    bp <cpu> add|list|remove ...

CONDITIONS
    An 'if <expr>' suffix turns a breakpoint into a conditional one: the
    address (or the access) still has to match, and then <expr> has to
    evaluate nonzero before execution actually halts. This is what makes a
    breakpoint usable on a routine that runs thousands of times a frame -
    'bp add 80A31C if x == 7' stops on the one iteration that matters
    instead of the first. See 'man eval' for the expression language; every
    symbol and memory form it documents works here unchanged.

    A condition reads what 'eval' reads at the same scope. An unscoped
    'bp add' reads the session's context, as an unscoped 'eval' does, and
    'bp <cpu> add' reads that processor's, as 'eval <cpu>' does; 'eval
    symbols' and 'eval <cpu> symbols' list the names each has. The
    registers are read as they stand at the hit, not as the last frame
    left them. This holds on every core, through either shell.

    The condition is checked when the breakpoint is set. One that does not
    parse, or that names a symbol its scope does not have, is refused
    there with status 1 and nothing is added:

        $ bp add 8008 if "a =="
        Error: Condition 'a ==' refused: Unexpected '<end>' in expression.
        Nothing was added.

    Conditions are evaluated on the emulation thread, once per matching
    hit, so an expensive one costs real frame time: each hit leaves the
    core's frame and re-enters it, and a condition that names a register
    first re-reads the registers: every processor's, and the video and
    sound rows. It does not re-read the console's views (palettes,
    sprites, channels), which no expression names; a pack's symbols, such
    as the SNES's flag.c, are worked out from the registers just read, and
    a unit's register (ppu.PPUCTRL) is read live, so every symbol is as it
    stands at the hit. Measured on 2026-10-18 on MoonRT, MercuryRT, MarsRT
    and Nephrite, that is a third to half a microsecond a hit for a
    condition of literals and about 1.0 to 1.4 for one naming a register;
    EmuSen_Backend.md, section 26.7, has the numbers. A breakpoint without
    a condition costs nothing more than it did. Reading an I/O register in
    a condition reads it for real, with whatever side effect that has on
    hardware - 'man eval' covers which reads are and aren't safe.

    A condition that passes the check can still fail when it is evaluated:
    a division by zero, or an address its space cannot read. Such a
    condition is DROPPED rather than left to fail on every hit: the
    breakpoint halts once, keeps its address, and loses its condition.
    Re-add the breakpoint once the expression is fixed.

    Quote a condition containing '&&', '||', '<', '>' or '|' - those are
    the shell's own operators and get consumed before this command ever
    sees them. Everything after the 'if' word is rejoined with single
    spaces, so an unquoted 'if a == 5' works fine.

DESCRIPTION
    Registers/lists/removes a breakpoint at a 24-bit CPU address. This
    command only edits the breakpoint list - it doesn't halt or resume
    execution itself, since that requires re-entering a core's own frame
    loop, which only the frontend's own main loop can do. See the F4
    prompt's own 'step'/'s' and 'continue'/'c' shortcuts (EmuSen.Hotaru) for
    the actual halt/resume side. Named 'bp', not 'break' - a command
    literally named 'break' would be unreachable, shadowed by the shell's
    own hardcoded break/continue loop-control keywords.

    'bp write' breaks on data instead of on control flow: it halts when
    anything writes <addr> in <space>, optionally only when the value
    written equals <value>. Where 'watch' records a write and carries on,
    this stops the machine there, so 'regs'/'mem' can read the state that
    produced it - the way to answer 'what was the source pointer' rather
    than just 'which PC stored it'. A write happens part-way through an
    instruction, so the halt lands on the instruction AFTER the store,
    exactly as a real debugger reports a data breakpoint.

    An optional 'sa1' (or 'cop') scope word in front of the subcommand
    targets a cartridge coprocessor's own CPU instead. The two live in
    separate lists because they are separate address spaces: an SA-1
    game's $00:82D7 is not the S-CPU's $00:82D7. Errors with 'no
    coprocessor CPU to break on' when the cartridge has none. Pair it
    with 'disasm SA1BUS <addr>', which decodes using the SA-1's own
    M/X/E flags rather than the S-CPU's.

    'bp read' is the mirror image, halting just after anything reads the
    address. Where 'readers' scans the ROM for instructions that COULD read
    something, this catches the read that actually happened, including ones
    through a computed pointer that no static scan can resolve - the way to
    find out who consumes a flag rather than who sets it.

    On a core of the core ABI that claims DEBUG_READS (the PlayStation's
    Diamond is the first), the halt lands IN FRONT of the loading
    instruction instead, nothing of it run: the core sees the load coming,
    so 'regs' shows the base register the address was made from and the
    destination before it is written. A value given is compared with the
    byte the space holds there just before the load. Resuming runs the
    load. On a core of the ABI that does not claim it, 'bp read' is
    accepted and never fires - see EmuSen_CoreAPI.md section 6.14.1.

    'bp on'/'bp off' toggle one breakpoint without losing its address,
    condition or hit count - the difference between silencing a breakpoint
    for one experiment and having to retype it afterwards.

    'bp list' resolves each address through the label registry, so a
    breakpoint on a named address reads '$00A3B2 <NmiHandler>'. See
    'man label'. A range prints as a range, since a label names one address.

RANGES
    Any address argument accepts '<start>-<end>' instead of one address, and
    the breakpoint then matches anywhere inside it. This is the difference
    between needing to already know the answer and being able to ask the
    question: 'bp write WRAM 0100-01FF' catches whoever is clobbering a
    struct when you do not yet know which byte of it moves first, and
    'bp add 818000-818FFF' catches whatever in a bank runs at all.

    The halt message names the exact address that matched as well as the
    range, so a range breakpoint tells you where it landed, not just that
    it landed.

CHANGED-ONLY WRITES
    'changed' on a write breakpoint suppresses the halt unless the byte
    written differs from the last one seen at that address. Games rewrite
    the same value constantly - a per-frame state byte re-stored every frame
    will trip a plain write breakpoint sixty times a second while telling
    you nothing. 'bp write WRAM 0DB3 changed' fires on the transition
    instead, which is almost always the moment being looked for.

    The first write to an address always counts as a change: there is no
    previous value to compare against, and a value written exactly once
    would otherwise never be reported at all. The comparison is against
    what this breakpoint last SAW, not against memory, so it costs no read
    and cannot disturb a side-effecting address.

UNINITIALIZED READS
    'bp uninit <space>' halts the first time the machine reads back a byte
    of <space> that nothing has written since the breakpoint was armed. That
    is a real defect nearly every time it happens: the game is consuming
    whatever the previous contents were, so its behavior depends on power-on
    garbage. It is also the single most useful check for the emulator
    itself, because it catches this core failing to initialize something
    that real hardware would have.

    Each address reports once and is then treated as initialized, or a boot
    loop over uninitialized memory would halt on the same byte forever.
    Arming allocates one bool per byte of the space and replaces any
    previous arming - one space is watched at a time. Note that a space
    written before arming counts as initialized; arm it early to catch
    reset-time reads. 'counters' reports the same thing as a whole-run
    tally when a halt is too blunt.

HARDWARE CONDITIONS
    'bp when <condition>' halts when the machine does something it should
    not, rather than when it reaches an address. 'bp when' with no argument
    lists what this core can detect, with each one's current state. Which
    conditions exist is each core's to publish, and since the C# SNES core
    was retired on 2026-10-16 no core publishes any: 'bp when' answers
    'This core reports no hardware conditions to break on.', status 1, on
    every core. Conditional breakpoints do not stand in for it - they test
    an expression where an address is reached, and a hardware condition is
    detected by the core wherever it happens. What the C# SNES core
    published, and what a core that publishes conditions again would be
    expected to offer, was:

        stp         the 65816 executed STP and is stopped until reset
        wdm         the 65816 executed WDM, a reserved opcode
        brk         a BRK was taken
        cop         a COP was taken
        ppuaccess   VRAM/CGRAM/OAM written while the display is rendering
        autojoy     $4218-$421F read while the auto-joypad read is running

    These matter because none of them are visible in the output. STP is the
    clearest case: a game that executes it has usually jumped somewhere it
    never meant to, and the symptom is simply that the picture stops - with
    no indication of where. 'bp when stp' halts on the instruction itself,
    while the call stack that got there is still readable with 'bt'.

    'ppuaccess' and 'autojoy' are the two where the emulator and the game
    disagree most quietly. Real hardware drops a VRAM write made during
    active display, and returns a half-updated byte from a joypad register
    read mid-refresh; a core that is more permissive than the hardware runs
    such a game correctly and hides a real bug, and a core that is less
    permissive breaks one that worked. Either way the first thing you want
    is to know it happened at all.

    A condition can be armed 'log' instead, which records every occurrence
    and carries on. That is the right mode for anything the game does often
    - a title screen may read the joypad early on every single frame, and
    halting on it tells you far less than seeing the pattern across frames.
    Logged conditions land in the same ring as logpoints and are read back
    with 'bp log', where they interleave with everything else in the order
    it really happened. 'bp when <condition> off' disarms one.

    Conditions cost nothing until one is armed: each detection site tests a
    single bool before computing anything.

DEPTH GUARD
    'bp depth <n>' halts as soon as the call stack gets deeper than <n>.
    Runaway recursion and a stack that never unwinds both present as a game
    that slows down and then behaves bizarrely, long after the actual defect
    - by the time anything visible goes wrong the evidence is gone. This
    stops the machine while the chain that caused it is still on the stack
    and readable with 'bt'. Fires once and disarms, so it will not retrigger
    on every instruction that stays deep. 'bp depth off' cancels it.

FORBID RANGES
    'bp forbid <start>-<end>' is a suppression rule, not a breakpoint:
    while the PC is anywhere inside the range, nothing halts - no address
    breakpoint, no data breakpoint, no uninitialized read. It exists for
    the case where the interesting breakpoint is drowned by one noisy
    caller: forbid the NMI handler's range and a watch on a shadow register
    reports only the game logic that writes it, not the per-frame flush.

    Steps are deliberately exempt. 'step' and 'step over' still work inside
    a forbidden range, or stepping into one would run away with no way to
    stop. Forbid ranges are listed by 'bp list' and removed by 'bp remove'
    like anything else, and 'bp off <id>' suspends one without losing it.

LOGPOINTS
    A 'log <expr>' clause turns a breakpoint into a logpoint: when it matches,
    <expr> is evaluated and recorded, and execution CARRIES ON. Read the
    recording back with 'bp log'.

    This is for the case a halting breakpoint cannot answer. A routine that
    runs four hundred times a frame cannot be stepped through four hundred
    times, and halting even once changes the timing of everything after it.
    'bp add 80A31C log "a, x, [$7E0DB3]"' records all four hundred and leaves
    the machine running at full speed, so the pattern across calls is visible
    rather than one arbitrary call being inspected in isolation.

    <expr> is a comma-separated list, and each part is rendered as 'expr=value'
    so a line names what it is showing. Quote it - commas are fine unquoted
    but the shell eats the brackets and operators that make it worth logging.
    Every symbol 'man eval' documents works, evaluated against the chip the
    breakpoint belongs to, at the scope and with the registers a condition
    reads (see CONDITIONS). Each part is checked when the logpoint is set,
    as a condition is, and one that does not parse or names an unknown
    symbol refuses the whole logpoint.

    A logpoint still honors 'if ', so the two compose: match the address,
    check the condition, then record instead of halting. A logpoint that
    fails to evaluate records the error text in place of the value rather
    than being dropped the way a bad condition is - a logpoint cannot fire
    forever on every instruction, so there is nothing to protect against.

    The log keeps the most recent 4096 entries and is shared by every
    logpoint on that processor, so entries interleave in the order they
    actually happened. 'bp log' shows the last 32 by default, 'bp log 100'
    more, and 'bp log clear' empties it. Data logpoints record at the moment
    of the access rather than one instruction later, so a logged write shows
    the value that was written.

EXAMPLES
    bp add 8000
    bp add 80A31C if x == 7
    bp add 80A31C log "a, x"
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 log "a, [$7E0100]" if "a > 4"
    bp log 100
    bp add 818000-818FFF
    bp add 808000 if "[$7E0020] != 0 && a > 16"
    bp write VRAM 2760
    bp write WRAM 0100-01FF
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 changed
    bp read WRAM 13c6
    bp uninit WRAM
    bp depth 40
    bp forbid 0080C4-0080FF
    bp off 1
    bp list
    bp sa1 add 0082D7
    bp gsu add 0A80E9 if "r14 > $100"

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word puts the breakpoint on another chip - see 'cpus' for the
    names. Each chip keeps its own breakpoint list, because each runs
    different code at the same addresses, and a condition attached to one
    is evaluated against THAT chip's registers: 'bp gsu add X if "r14 > 0"'
    reads the GSU's R14, not anything on the main CPU, and 'bp z80 add 1
    if "AF>>8 == 5"' on the Genesis reads the Z80's A, where the same
    condition unscoped is refused because the 68000 has no AF.

    A chip the core cannot halt takes no breakpoints and says so rather
    than accepting one that would never fire.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, eval, step, runto, bt, watch, cov, counters, readers, writers
status 0
action none
$ man Bp
out 15811
NAME
    bp - manage execution breakpoints

SYNOPSIS
    bp add <addr>[-<end>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp write <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [changed] [if <expr>]
    bp read <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [if <expr>]
    bp uninit <space>
    bp when [<condition>] [log|off]
    bp depth <n>|off
    bp forbid <addr>-<end>
    bp log [<count>] | bp log clear
    bp list
    bp on|off <id>
    bp remove <id>
    bp <cpu> add|list|remove ...

CONDITIONS
    An 'if <expr>' suffix turns a breakpoint into a conditional one: the
    address (or the access) still has to match, and then <expr> has to
    evaluate nonzero before execution actually halts. This is what makes a
    breakpoint usable on a routine that runs thousands of times a frame -
    'bp add 80A31C if x == 7' stops on the one iteration that matters
    instead of the first. See 'man eval' for the expression language; every
    symbol and memory form it documents works here unchanged.

    A condition reads what 'eval' reads at the same scope. An unscoped
    'bp add' reads the session's context, as an unscoped 'eval' does, and
    'bp <cpu> add' reads that processor's, as 'eval <cpu>' does; 'eval
    symbols' and 'eval <cpu> symbols' list the names each has. The
    registers are read as they stand at the hit, not as the last frame
    left them. This holds on every core, through either shell.

    The condition is checked when the breakpoint is set. One that does not
    parse, or that names a symbol its scope does not have, is refused
    there with status 1 and nothing is added:

        $ bp add 8008 if "a =="
        Error: Condition 'a ==' refused: Unexpected '<end>' in expression.
        Nothing was added.

    Conditions are evaluated on the emulation thread, once per matching
    hit, so an expensive one costs real frame time: each hit leaves the
    core's frame and re-enters it, and a condition that names a register
    first re-reads the registers: every processor's, and the video and
    sound rows. It does not re-read the console's views (palettes,
    sprites, channels), which no expression names; a pack's symbols, such
    as the SNES's flag.c, are worked out from the registers just read, and
    a unit's register (ppu.PPUCTRL) is read live, so every symbol is as it
    stands at the hit. Measured on 2026-10-18 on MoonRT, MercuryRT, MarsRT
    and Nephrite, that is a third to half a microsecond a hit for a
    condition of literals and about 1.0 to 1.4 for one naming a register;
    EmuSen_Backend.md, section 26.7, has the numbers. A breakpoint without
    a condition costs nothing more than it did. Reading an I/O register in
    a condition reads it for real, with whatever side effect that has on
    hardware - 'man eval' covers which reads are and aren't safe.

    A condition that passes the check can still fail when it is evaluated:
    a division by zero, or an address its space cannot read. Such a
    condition is DROPPED rather than left to fail on every hit: the
    breakpoint halts once, keeps its address, and loses its condition.
    Re-add the breakpoint once the expression is fixed.

    Quote a condition containing '&&', '||', '<', '>' or '|' - those are
    the shell's own operators and get consumed before this command ever
    sees them. Everything after the 'if' word is rejoined with single
    spaces, so an unquoted 'if a == 5' works fine.

DESCRIPTION
    Registers/lists/removes a breakpoint at a 24-bit CPU address. This
    command only edits the breakpoint list - it doesn't halt or resume
    execution itself, since that requires re-entering a core's own frame
    loop, which only the frontend's own main loop can do. See the F4
    prompt's own 'step'/'s' and 'continue'/'c' shortcuts (EmuSen.Hotaru) for
    the actual halt/resume side. Named 'bp', not 'break' - a command
    literally named 'break' would be unreachable, shadowed by the shell's
    own hardcoded break/continue loop-control keywords.

    'bp write' breaks on data instead of on control flow: it halts when
    anything writes <addr> in <space>, optionally only when the value
    written equals <value>. Where 'watch' records a write and carries on,
    this stops the machine there, so 'regs'/'mem' can read the state that
    produced it - the way to answer 'what was the source pointer' rather
    than just 'which PC stored it'. A write happens part-way through an
    instruction, so the halt lands on the instruction AFTER the store,
    exactly as a real debugger reports a data breakpoint.

    An optional 'sa1' (or 'cop') scope word in front of the subcommand
    targets a cartridge coprocessor's own CPU instead. The two live in
    separate lists because they are separate address spaces: an SA-1
    game's $00:82D7 is not the S-CPU's $00:82D7. Errors with 'no
    coprocessor CPU to break on' when the cartridge has none. Pair it
    with 'disasm SA1BUS <addr>', which decodes using the SA-1's own
    M/X/E flags rather than the S-CPU's.

