case the page for man
target none
covers 1
$ man man
out 555
NAME
    man - show a command's manual page

SYNOPSIS
    man [command]

DESCRIPTION
    The detail lookup: with a command name, prints that command's full
    manual page (this text) instead of just its one-line usage. With no
    argument, there's nothing else useful to show, so it falls back to the
    same overview 'help' prints. 'help' and 'man' are two separate commands,
    not aliases of each other - 'help' always lists everything and never
    shows a single command's full page; 'man' is the other way around.

EXAMPLES
    man
    man watch
status 0
action none
end

case the page for help
target none
covers 1
$ man help
out 317
NAME
    help - list every command with a brief explanation

SYNOPSIS
    help

DESCRIPTION
    Prints every command, one line each, with a brief explanation of what it
    does - the quick "what's available" overview. Ignores any arguments;
    for a specific command's full manual page, use 'man <command>' instead.
status 0
action none
end

case the page for summary
target none
covers 1
$ man summary
out 722
NAME
    summary - free-text state dump

SYNOPSIS
    summary

DESCRIPTION
    Prints whatever the current IDebugTarget's GetSummaryText() has to say -
    a catch-all, unstructured dump for state that doesn't have its own
    dedicated command yet. 'No target attached.' if no ROM is loaded.

    On a core of the stable ABI the first line names the engine, the system
    and the frame, and the console's system pack adds what it reads from the
    main processor's live registers. On the SNES that is the 65816's state on
    one line, P's letters upper case where set, the same line C# Venus prints
    first; the PPU lines Venus adds need the video registers, which no SNES
    engine of the stable ABI publishes yet.
status 0
action none
end

case the page for export
target none
covers 1
$ man export
out 625
NAME
    export - set a shell variable

SYNOPSIS
    export NAME[=value]

DESCRIPTION
    Real bash exports a variable into child processes' environment; there is
    no such thing here (this shell never spawns a subprocess), so 'export' is
    just a plain variable assignment - or a no-op declaration if no '=value'
    is given and the name is already set. Kept purely so a script written
    with real bash habits ('export FOO=bar') still does something sane
    instead of erroring as an unknown command. Plain 'NAME=value' (no
    'export' keyword) works exactly the same way.

EXAMPLES
    export FOO=bar
    echo $FOO
status 0
action none
end

case the page for unset
target none
covers 1
$ man unset
out 218
NAME
    unset - remove a shell variable

SYNOPSIS
    unset NAME

DESCRIPTION
    Removes NAME from this shell's variables. Referencing an unset variable
    afterward ($NAME) expands to an empty string, same as bash.
status 0
action none
end

case the page for source
target none
covers 1
$ man source
out 1256
NAME
    source, . - run a script file in this shell's own scope

SYNOPSIS
    source <path>
    . <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Runs <path>'s lines one at a time, exactly as if each had been typed at
    the prompt - including multi-line if/for/while blocks spanning several
    physical lines in the file. Runs in THIS shell's own variable scope, not
    an isolated subshell: a variable the script sets is still set once
    'source' returns, matching real bash 'source' (and unlike $(...) command
    substitution, which does run in an isolated subshell - see 'man' on
    command substitution... there isn't one; see the shell reference doc).
    Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are skipped. A script's own
    lines are NOT recorded into 'history'/!N recall, and '!'/'!!' expansion
    is disabled for them - only the 'source' invocation itself shows up in
    history, matching real bash. Walled to the project's own directory tree
    (the same sandbox 'cd'/'ls'/'mv' use) - a path outside it is rejected.
    A script ending mid-block, or a self-referential/mutually-recursive
    'source' chain (capped at 20 nested levels), reports a clean error
    instead of hanging the console or crashing it.

EXAMPLES
    source setup.txt
    . setup.txt
status 0
action none
end

case the page for if
target none
covers 1
$ man if
out 595
NAME
    if - conditional execution

SYNOPSIS
    if LIST; then LIST; [elif LIST; then LIST;] ... [else LIST;] fi

DESCRIPTION
    Runs the 'if' condition; if its last pipeline exits 0 (success), runs the
    'then' body and stops. Otherwise tries each 'elif' condition in turn the
    same way, finally falling through to 'else' if none matched. Works
    across multiple physical lines at the interactive prompt (a secondary
    '> ' prompt appears until 'fi' closes it) and inside a 'source'd script
    the same way.

EXAMPLES
    if [ -f save.state ]; then echo found; else echo missing; fi
status 0
action none
end

case the page for for
target none
covers 1
$ man for
out 518
NAME
    for - loop over a list of words

SYNOPSIS
    for NAME in word...; do LIST; done

DESCRIPTION
    Sets variable NAME to each word in turn (already expanded - quoting,
    $VAR, and $(...) all apply to the word list) and runs the body once per
    word. 'break' exits the loop early; 'continue' skips to the next word.
    Every iteration counts against a shared 10-second wall-clock safety
    timeout (see 'while') that aborts a runaway loop with a clear error.

EXAMPLES
    for i in 1 2 3; do echo $i; done
status 0
action none
end

case the page for while
target none
covers 1
$ man while
out 758
NAME
    while, until - loop while/until a condition holds

SYNOPSIS
    while LIST; do LIST; done
    until LIST; do LIST; done

DESCRIPTION
    'while' repeats its body as long as the condition's last pipeline exits
    0; 'until' repeats as long as it does NOT (i.e. until it succeeds). Same
    'break'/'continue' as 'for'. A shared 10-second wall-clock budget (also
    counted against by any nested $(...) command substitution, so it can't
    be bypassed that way) aborts a typo'd infinite loop with a clear error
    instead of hanging the console - a real 'while true; do echo x; done'
    prints for about 10 seconds, then cleanly reports the timeout.

EXAMPLES
    while false; do echo never; done
    until [ -f ready.txt ]; do echo waiting; done
status 0
action none
end

case the page for break
target none
covers 1
$ man break
out 547
NAME
    break - exit the nearest enclosing loop

SYNOPSIS
    break

DESCRIPTION
    A hardcoded, zero-argument parser keyword (not an ordinary dispatched
    command), same as real bash's own break/continue builtins. Ends the
    nearest enclosing 'for'/'while'/'until' loop immediately. Outside any
    loop it's a silent no-op, matching bash. Note: the breakpoint command is
    named 'bp', not 'break' - a command literally named 'break' would be
    unreachable, since this keyword is recognized before any command lookup
    happens at all.
status 0
action none
end

