case the page for watch
target none
covers 1
$ man watch
out 1471
NAME
    watch - track reads/writes to a memory range

SYNOPSIS
    watch add <space> <addr> <len> [write|read|both]
    watch list
    watch log <id> [<count>]
    watch summary <id>
    watch clear <id>
    watch remove <id>

DESCRIPTION
    'add' registers a watch over <addr>..<addr>+<len>-1 in <space> (default
    kind: write) and starts recording matching accesses as they happen -
    this is a LIVE, triggered mechanism (see 'framelog' for the sampled-
    every-frame alternative, and 'callers'/'writers'/'readers' for static
    analysis that doesn't require the access to actually happen first).
    'log' shows a watch's recorded (sequence, kind, address, value, context)
    events, newest last. 'summary' groups those events by access site
    instead of listing every one - the dynamic, addressing-mode-agnostic
    equivalent of 'readers'/'writers'. 'clear' empties a watch's recorded
    events without removing the watch itself; 'remove' deletes it entirely.

    On a core of the core ABI, a read or both watch sees loads only where
    the core claims DEBUG_READS, and only in the spaces it reports loads
    for (on the PlayStation: R3000BUS, RAM and SCRATCH); elsewhere a read
    watch records nothing and a both watch records the stores. Each
    event's context is the loading instruction's address - see
    EmuSen_CoreAPI.md section 6.14.1.

EXAMPLES
    watch add WRAM 13c6 1
    watch add RAM 20000 4 read
    watch log 1
    watch summary 1
status 0
action none
end

case the page for bp
target none
covers 1
$ man bp
out 15811
NAME
    bp - manage execution breakpoints

SYNOPSIS
    bp add <addr>[-<end>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp write <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [changed] [if <expr>]
    bp read <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [if <expr>]
    bp uninit <space>
    bp when [<condition>] [log|off]
    bp depth <n>|off
    bp forbid <addr>-<end>
    bp log [<count>] | bp log clear
    bp list
    bp on|off <id>
    bp remove <id>
    bp <cpu> add|list|remove ...

CONDITIONS
    An 'if <expr>' suffix turns a breakpoint into a conditional one: the
    address (or the access) still has to match, and then <expr> has to
    evaluate nonzero before execution actually halts. This is what makes a
    breakpoint usable on a routine that runs thousands of times a frame -
    'bp add 80A31C if x == 7' stops on the one iteration that matters
    instead of the first. See 'man eval' for the expression language; every
    symbol and memory form it documents works here unchanged.

    A condition reads what 'eval' reads at the same scope. An unscoped
    'bp add' reads the session's context, as an unscoped 'eval' does, and
    'bp <cpu> add' reads that processor's, as 'eval <cpu>' does; 'eval
    symbols' and 'eval <cpu> symbols' list the names each has. The
    registers are read as they stand at the hit, not as the last frame
    left them. This holds on every core, through either shell.

    The condition is checked when the breakpoint is set. One that does not
    parse, or that names a symbol its scope does not have, is refused
    there with status 1 and nothing is added:

        $ bp add 8008 if "a =="
        Error: Condition 'a ==' refused: Unexpected '<end>' in expression.
        Nothing was added.

    Conditions are evaluated on the emulation thread, once per matching
    hit, so an expensive one costs real frame time: each hit leaves the
    core's frame and re-enters it, and a condition that names a register
    first re-reads the registers: every processor's, and the video and
    sound rows. It does not re-read the console's views (palettes,
    sprites, channels), which no expression names; a pack's symbols, such
    as the SNES's flag.c, are worked out from the registers just read, and
    a unit's register (ppu.PPUCTRL) is read live, so every symbol is as it
    stands at the hit. Measured on 2026-10-18 on MoonRT, MercuryRT, MarsRT
    and Nephrite, that is a third to half a microsecond a hit for a
    condition of literals and about 1.0 to 1.4 for one naming a register;
    EmuSen_Backend.md, section 26.7, has the numbers. A breakpoint without
    a condition costs nothing more than it did. Reading an I/O register in
    a condition reads it for real, with whatever side effect that has on
    hardware - 'man eval' covers which reads are and aren't safe.

    A condition that passes the check can still fail when it is evaluated:
    a division by zero, or an address its space cannot read. Such a
    condition is DROPPED rather than left to fail on every hit: the
    breakpoint halts once, keeps its address, and loses its condition.
    Re-add the breakpoint once the expression is fixed.

    Quote a condition containing '&&', '||', '<', '>' or '|' - those are
    the shell's own operators and get consumed before this command ever
    sees them. Everything after the 'if' word is rejoined with single
    spaces, so an unquoted 'if a == 5' works fine.

DESCRIPTION
    Registers/lists/removes a breakpoint at a 24-bit CPU address. This
    command only edits the breakpoint list - it doesn't halt or resume
    execution itself, since that requires re-entering a core's own frame
    loop, which only the frontend's own main loop can do. See the F4
    prompt's own 'step'/'s' and 'continue'/'c' shortcuts (EmuSen.Hotaru) for
    the actual halt/resume side. Named 'bp', not 'break' - a command
    literally named 'break' would be unreachable, shadowed by the shell's
    own hardcoded break/continue loop-control keywords.

    'bp write' breaks on data instead of on control flow: it halts when
    anything writes <addr> in <space>, optionally only when the value
    written equals <value>. Where 'watch' records a write and carries on,
    this stops the machine there, so 'regs'/'mem' can read the state that
    produced it - the way to answer 'what was the source pointer' rather
    than just 'which PC stored it'. A write happens part-way through an
    instruction, so the halt lands on the instruction AFTER the store,
    exactly as a real debugger reports a data breakpoint.

    An optional 'sa1' (or 'cop') scope word in front of the subcommand
    targets a cartridge coprocessor's own CPU instead. The two live in
    separate lists because they are separate address spaces: an SA-1
    game's $00:82D7 is not the S-CPU's $00:82D7. Errors with 'no
    coprocessor CPU to break on' when the cartridge has none. Pair it
    with 'disasm SA1BUS <addr>', which decodes using the SA-1's own
    M/X/E flags rather than the S-CPU's.

    'bp read' is the mirror image, halting just after anything reads the
    address. Where 'readers' scans the ROM for instructions that COULD read
    something, this catches the read that actually happened, including ones
    through a computed pointer that no static scan can resolve - the way to
    find out who consumes a flag rather than who sets it.

    On a core of the core ABI that claims DEBUG_READS (the PlayStation's
    Diamond is the first), the halt lands IN FRONT of the loading
    instruction instead, nothing of it run: the core sees the load coming,
    so 'regs' shows the base register the address was made from and the
    destination before it is written. A value given is compared with the
    byte the space holds there just before the load. Resuming runs the
    load. On a core of the ABI that does not claim it, 'bp read' is
    accepted and never fires - see EmuSen_CoreAPI.md section 6.14.1.

    'bp on'/'bp off' toggle one breakpoint without losing its address,
    condition or hit count - the difference between silencing a breakpoint
    for one experiment and having to retype it afterwards.

    'bp list' resolves each address through the label registry, so a
    breakpoint on a named address reads '$00A3B2 <NmiHandler>'. See
    'man label'. A range prints as a range, since a label names one address.

RANGES
    Any address argument accepts '<start>-<end>' instead of one address, and
    the breakpoint then matches anywhere inside it. This is the difference
    between needing to already know the answer and being able to ask the
    question: 'bp write WRAM 0100-01FF' catches whoever is clobbering a
    struct when you do not yet know which byte of it moves first, and
    'bp add 818000-818FFF' catches whatever in a bank runs at all.

    The halt message names the exact address that matched as well as the
    range, so a range breakpoint tells you where it landed, not just that
    it landed.

CHANGED-ONLY WRITES
    'changed' on a write breakpoint suppresses the halt unless the byte
    written differs from the last one seen at that address. Games rewrite
    the same value constantly - a per-frame state byte re-stored every frame
    will trip a plain write breakpoint sixty times a second while telling
    you nothing. 'bp write WRAM 0DB3 changed' fires on the transition
    instead, which is almost always the moment being looked for.

    The first write to an address always counts as a change: there is no
    previous value to compare against, and a value written exactly once
    would otherwise never be reported at all. The comparison is against
    what this breakpoint last SAW, not against memory, so it costs no read
    and cannot disturb a side-effecting address.

UNINITIALIZED READS
    'bp uninit <space>' halts the first time the machine reads back a byte
    of <space> that nothing has written since the breakpoint was armed. That
    is a real defect nearly every time it happens: the game is consuming
    whatever the previous contents were, so its behavior depends on power-on
    garbage. It is also the single most useful check for the emulator
    itself, because it catches this core failing to initialize something
    that real hardware would have.

    Each address reports once and is then treated as initialized, or a boot
    loop over uninitialized memory would halt on the same byte forever.
    Arming allocates one bool per byte of the space and replaces any
    previous arming - one space is watched at a time. Note that a space
    written before arming counts as initialized; arm it early to catch
    reset-time reads. 'counters' reports the same thing as a whole-run
    tally when a halt is too blunt.

HARDWARE CONDITIONS
    'bp when <condition>' halts when the machine does something it should
    not, rather than when it reaches an address. 'bp when' with no argument
    lists what this core can detect, with each one's current state. Which
    conditions exist is each core's to publish, and since the C# SNES core
    was retired on 2026-10-16 no core publishes any: 'bp when' answers
    'This core reports no hardware conditions to break on.', status 1, on
    every core. Conditional breakpoints do not stand in for it - they test
    an expression where an address is reached, and a hardware condition is
    detected by the core wherever it happens. What the C# SNES core
    published, and what a core that publishes conditions again would be
    expected to offer, was:

        stp         the 65816 executed STP and is stopped until reset
        wdm         the 65816 executed WDM, a reserved opcode
        brk         a BRK was taken
        cop         a COP was taken
        ppuaccess   VRAM/CGRAM/OAM written while the display is rendering
        autojoy     $4218-$421F read while the auto-joypad read is running

    These matter because none of them are visible in the output. STP is the
    clearest case: a game that executes it has usually jumped somewhere it
    never meant to, and the symptom is simply that the picture stops - with
    no indication of where. 'bp when stp' halts on the instruction itself,
    while the call stack that got there is still readable with 'bt'.

    'ppuaccess' and 'autojoy' are the two where the emulator and the game
    disagree most quietly. Real hardware drops a VRAM write made during
    active display, and returns a half-updated byte from a joypad register
    read mid-refresh; a core that is more permissive than the hardware runs
    such a game correctly and hides a real bug, and a core that is less
    permissive breaks one that worked. Either way the first thing you want
    is to know it happened at all.

    A condition can be armed 'log' instead, which records every occurrence
    and carries on. That is the right mode for anything the game does often
    - a title screen may read the joypad early on every single frame, and
    halting on it tells you far less than seeing the pattern across frames.
    Logged conditions land in the same ring as logpoints and are read back
    with 'bp log', where they interleave with everything else in the order
    it really happened. 'bp when <condition> off' disarms one.

    Conditions cost nothing until one is armed: each detection site tests a
    single bool before computing anything.

DEPTH GUARD
    'bp depth <n>' halts as soon as the call stack gets deeper than <n>.
    Runaway recursion and a stack that never unwinds both present as a game
    that slows down and then behaves bizarrely, long after the actual defect
    - by the time anything visible goes wrong the evidence is gone. This
    stops the machine while the chain that caused it is still on the stack
    and readable with 'bt'. Fires once and disarms, so it will not retrigger
    on every instruction that stays deep. 'bp depth off' cancels it.

FORBID RANGES
    'bp forbid <start>-<end>' is a suppression rule, not a breakpoint:
    while the PC is anywhere inside the range, nothing halts - no address
    breakpoint, no data breakpoint, no uninitialized read. It exists for
    the case where the interesting breakpoint is drowned by one noisy
    caller: forbid the NMI handler's range and a watch on a shadow register
    reports only the game logic that writes it, not the per-frame flush.

    Steps are deliberately exempt. 'step' and 'step over' still work inside
    a forbidden range, or stepping into one would run away with no way to
    stop. Forbid ranges are listed by 'bp list' and removed by 'bp remove'
    like anything else, and 'bp off <id>' suspends one without losing it.

LOGPOINTS
    A 'log <expr>' clause turns a breakpoint into a logpoint: when it matches,
    <expr> is evaluated and recorded, and execution CARRIES ON. Read the
    recording back with 'bp log'.

    This is for the case a halting breakpoint cannot answer. A routine that
    runs four hundred times a frame cannot be stepped through four hundred
    times, and halting even once changes the timing of everything after it.
    'bp add 80A31C log "a, x, [$7E0DB3]"' records all four hundred and leaves
    the machine running at full speed, so the pattern across calls is visible
    rather than one arbitrary call being inspected in isolation.

    <expr> is a comma-separated list, and each part is rendered as 'expr=value'
    so a line names what it is showing. Quote it - commas are fine unquoted
    but the shell eats the brackets and operators that make it worth logging.
    Every symbol 'man eval' documents works, evaluated against the chip the
    breakpoint belongs to, at the scope and with the registers a condition
    reads (see CONDITIONS). Each part is checked when the logpoint is set,
    as a condition is, and one that does not parse or names an unknown
    symbol refuses the whole logpoint.

    A logpoint still honors 'if ', so the two compose: match the address,
    check the condition, then record instead of halting. A logpoint that
    fails to evaluate records the error text in place of the value rather
    than being dropped the way a bad condition is - a logpoint cannot fire
    forever on every instruction, so there is nothing to protect against.

    The log keeps the most recent 4096 entries and is shared by every
    logpoint on that processor, so entries interleave in the order they
    actually happened. 'bp log' shows the last 32 by default, 'bp log 100'
    more, and 'bp log clear' empties it. Data logpoints record at the moment
    of the access rather than one instruction later, so a logged write shows
    the value that was written.

EXAMPLES
    bp add 8000
    bp add 80A31C if x == 7
    bp add 80A31C log "a, x"
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 log "a, [$7E0100]" if "a > 4"
    bp log 100
    bp add 818000-818FFF
    bp add 808000 if "[$7E0020] != 0 && a > 16"
    bp write VRAM 2760
    bp write WRAM 0100-01FF
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 changed
    bp read WRAM 13c6
    bp uninit WRAM
    bp depth 40
    bp forbid 0080C4-0080FF
    bp off 1
    bp list
    bp sa1 add 0082D7
    bp gsu add 0A80E9 if "r14 > $100"

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word puts the breakpoint on another chip - see 'cpus' for the
    names. Each chip keeps its own breakpoint list, because each runs
    different code at the same addresses, and a condition attached to one
    is evaluated against THAT chip's registers: 'bp gsu add X if "r14 > 0"'
    reads the GSU's R14, not anything on the main CPU, and 'bp z80 add 1
    if "AF>>8 == 5"' on the Genesis reads the Z80's A, where the same
    condition unscoped is refused because the 68000 has no AF.

    A chip the core cannot halt takes no breakpoints and says so rather
    than accepting one that would never fire.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, eval, step, runto, bt, watch, cov, counters, readers, writers
status 0
action none
end

case the page for setreg
target none
covers 1
$ man setreg
out 2243
NAME
    setreg - write a CPU register

SYNOPSIS
    setreg <register> <value>
    setreg <cpu> <register> <value>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets one of a processor's registers to a value. Until this existed the
    debugger could read every register and change none of them, which meant
    a hypothesis could only ever be tested by editing memory and waiting to
    see whether the machine happened to reload the register from it.

    What it is actually for is counterfactuals. Halt on the compare that
    gates a routine, flip the register it compares, resume, and watch what
    the game does down the branch it did not take. That answers 'is this
    check the reason my sprite never appears' in one step, where coverage
    and breakpoints can only establish that the branch was not taken.

    It also skips a hang: forcing PC past a spin loop lets a boot sequence
    continue far enough to show what the NEXT problem is, which is often
    worth more than the one you are stopped on.

    Values are hex, and are REFUSED rather than truncated if they do not fit
    the register's width - a silently masked value would be a wrong answer
    reported as a success. An unknown register name lists the ones that chip
    actually has.

    Kept separate from 'regs' on purpose. 'regs' is read-only, which is what
    lets an always-live shell answer it instantly off the emulation thread;
    folding a write into it would drag every register dump onto the
    emulation thread's next frame, and writing a register from another
    thread would be a race besides.

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word writes another chip's registers - 'cpus' lists which ones
    support it at all. Not every chip does: the GSU and the NEC DSP report
    their registers through debug accessors with no write path behind them,
    so they refuse rather than pretending. 'cpus' prints 'setreg' in a
    chip's supported list only when it really is writable.

    Take care with a halted coprocessor: only one chip halts at a time, so
    writing a register on a chip that is still running races its own
    execution.

EXAMPLES
    setreg a 0007
    setreg pc 8123
    setreg p 34
    setreg spc a 00
    setreg sa1 x 0010

SEE ALSO
    regs, cpus, bp, step, eval
status 0
action none
end

case the page for vectors
target none
covers 1
$ man vectors
out 1444
NAME
    vectors - where each interrupt vector points

SYNOPSIS
    vectors

DESCRIPTION
    Reads the interrupt vector table out of the running machine and shows
    what each entry points at, resolved through the label registry. 'Which
    routine handles NMI' is a question that comes up in almost every
    investigation, and the alternative is remembering that the SNES keeps
    its native NMI vector at $00FFEA and hand-reading two bytes.

    Vectors are grouped by CPU mode, because a 65816 has two full sets and
    which one the hardware uses depends on the E flag at the moment the
    interrupt is taken. 'regs' shows E. A game that sets up only the native
    table and then takes an interrupt in emulation mode jumps somewhere it
    never intended, and seeing both tables side by side is what makes that
    visible.

    When coverage recording is on ('man cov'), a vector whose target has
    actually executed is marked '(ran)'. A vector that points at plausible
    code which never ran is a much stronger signal than one that merely
    looks wrong - it says the interrupt is not firing at all, rather than
    firing and doing the wrong thing.

    The pointers are read live through the CpuBus, so a game that rewrites
    its own vector table (or maps different ROM in) reports what is mapped
    right now, not what was there at reset.

EXAMPLES
    vectors
    cov on; vectors

SEE ALSO
    runto, bt, cov, label, addr
status 0
action none
end

case the page for addr
target none
covers 1
$ man addr
out 1413
NAME
    addr - decode a CPU-bus address

SYNOPSIS
    addr <addr>

DESCRIPTION
    Says what a 24-bit CPU-bus address actually reaches: a ROM file offset,
    a RAM offset, or a hardware register. The CPU's view and the ROM file's
    layout are different coordinate systems, and every task that crosses
    between them - comparing against a ROM in a hex editor, writing a patch,
    reading a disassembly someone else produced - needs the translation.
    Doing it by hand means knowing the mapper, which is exactly the detail
    worth not having to remember.

    The decode is the cartridge's own, the same one a real read goes
    through, so it is right for whatever mapper this ROM uses rather than
    assuming LoROM. ROM offsets are reported modulo the ROM's real size,
    matching how an undersized ROM mirrors on hardware.

    Addressable results print a ready-made 'mem' command underneath, since
    looking at the bytes is almost always the next thing wanted. A hardware
    register reports as a register rather than being given a fake offset -
    there is nothing there to point 'mem' at.

    An address the cartridge does not map and the bus does not claim
    reports as unmapped, which is itself an answer: a pointer that decodes
    to nothing is a pointer that was computed wrong.

EXAMPLES
    addr 808000
    addr 7E0DB3
    addr 002100

SEE ALSO
    mem, dump, spaces, vectors, cheat
status 0
action none
end

case the page for cov
target none
covers 1
$ man cov
out 4246
NAME
    cov - record which code actually ran

SYNOPSIS
    cov on|off
    cov clear
    cov <addr> [<len>]
    cov mark
    cov new <addr> [<len>]
    cov funcs [<count>]
    cov save|load <file>
    cov <cpu> on|off|clear|<addr> [<len>]

DESCRIPTION
    Records every 24-bit address executed between 'cov on' and 'cov off',
    then answers 'did control flow ever reach here' for any range. A
    breakpoint can only say whether execution is at an address right now,
    so a breakpoint that never fires proves nothing on its own; this says
    outright that a routine never ran, which is the answer that retires a
    suspect. Pair it with 'callers': walk up from a routine that never ran
    until you reach a caller that did, and the branch between the two is
    the one that skipped it.

    Reports executed addresses, not instruction boundaries you guessed at -
    so it also settles where 'disasm' has mis-sized an immediate, since the
    recorded addresses are the real opcode boundaries.

    Recording is off by default and costs one bool test while disarmed;
    armed, it allocates a 2MB bitmap per processor recorded.

DIFFERENTIAL COVERAGE
    'cov mark' freezes what has run so far; 'cov new <addr>' then reports
    only what has run SINCE. This is the sharpest tool here, because it
    converts a question about code into a question about the machine: mark,
    press the button (open the door, take the damage, trigger the glitch),
    and every address 'cov new' reports is code that ran because of what
    you just did. Whole-run coverage cannot separate that from the boot
    sequence and the per-frame loop, which together dwarf it.

    Marking does not stop or clear recording, so several marks in a session
    just move the baseline forward. 'cov clear' drops the mark along with
    everything else. A mark costs a second 2MB bitmap.

DISCOVERED ROUTINES
    'cov funcs' lists every address a call actually landed on while
    recording, with how many times each was entered and whether it was
    reached by a call or by an interrupt vector. These are real entry
    points, observed rather than inferred - a static scan finds routines
    reached by a literal JSR, and misses every one reached through a jump
    table or a computed pointer, which on a SNES game is most of them.

    Output is designed to be pasted into 'label': the addresses are exactly
    the ones worth naming, and naming them makes every later 'bt', 'bp
    list' and 'profile' readable. Where 'profile' ranks routines by the
    time they cost, this enumerates them, including the cheap ones.

PERSISTENCE
    'cov save <file>' writes the bitmap and the discovered routines to
    Logs/<CoreName>/<file>; 'cov load' MERGES a saved map back in rather
    than replacing what is recorded, so maps from several sessions
    accumulate into one. A map of a whole playthrough is worth far more
    than a map of one sitting, and no single sitting reaches every
    routine. Because it adds, a routine's entry counts are the sum over
    every map loaded: the same map loaded twice counts each entry twice.

    Name it with a .covmap extension - the repo gitignores that, and a map
    is a recording of one person's playthrough rather than source.

    The file is a fixed header, the routine table, then the raw bitmap.
    It is keyed to nothing - loading a map taken from a different ROM will
    quietly merge nonsense, so keep the filename tied to the game.

    The name is resolved as 'dump' and 'load' resolve theirs: from
    Logs/<CoreName>/, or from the root with a leading '/', and refused if
    it lands above the root - see 'man dump'.

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word records another chip's instruction stream instead - see
    'man cpus' for the names. Each chip gets its own bitmap, because each
    runs its own code: an address covered on the SA-1 says nothing about
    whether the main CPU ever executed the same number. 'cop' still works
    as an alias for whichever cartridge coprocessor is present.

EXAMPLES
    cov on
    cov 10F452 10
    cov mark
    cov new 108000 8000
    cov funcs 20
    cov save alttp.covmap
    cov gsu 0A80E9 40
    cov spc on

SEE ALSO
    cpus, bp, callers, disasm, label, profile
status 0
action none
end

case the page for framelog
target none
covers 1
$ man framelog
out 644
NAME
    framelog - sample a memory value once per frame

SYNOPSIS
    framelog add <space> <addr> [<width>]
    framelog list
    framelog show <id> [<count>]
    framelog clear <id>
    framelog remove <id>

DESCRIPTION
    Unlike 'watch' (triggered by an actual read/write), a frame log samples
    its value once every frame regardless of whether anything touched it -
    useful for 'what does this value do over time' questions a write watch
    can't answer, e.g. a counter written once at level start but read every
    frame afterward. <width> is 1, 2, or 4 bytes (default 1).

EXAMPLES
    framelog add WRAM 1fb0 2
    framelog show 1
status 0
action none
end

case the page for memcard
target none
covers 1
$ man memcard
out 3861
NAME
    memcard - manage memory cards, paks and a console's save memory

SYNOPSIS
    memcard ls [<card>]
    memcard cp <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard mv <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard rm <card>:<save>
    memcard import <card> <from>...
    memcard new <card>
    memcard dup <card> <new card>
    memcard rename <card> <new card>

DESCRIPTION
    The card manager of every console that keeps saves on a medium of its
    own: the PlayStation's and the PlayStation 2's memory cards, and as
    their cores declare them the N64's Controller Paks and the Sega CD's
    backup RAM. A <card> is a file in the sandbox, named as 'ls' and 'cp'
    name it:

        Saves/PlayStation/Cards/Card 1.mcr     a shared or named card
        Saves/PlayStation/Amber Saga.mcr       a game's own card
        Saves/PlayStation 2/Cards/Card 1.ps2   a PlayStation 2 card
        Saves/Sega CD/Console/Backup RAM 1.brm the console's own memory

    Its medium is known by its extension, from the console's system pack.
    A <save> is the save's name on the card, or its number in 'memcard ls'.

    'ls' with no card lists every card under Saves by console, with its
    medium, its free space and whether a game holds it; with a card, the
    saves on it, each with its size, its name and its title. 'cp' copies
    a save onto another card of the same medium; 'mv' copies it, reads it
    back from the second card, and only then deletes it from the first.
    'rm' deletes a save as the console's own manager does: the PlayStation
    card's directory marks it deleted and keeps its blocks; a PlayStation 2
    save is a directory, removed with every file in it.

    'import' copies every save of the <from> cards onto <card>, ALL OR
    NONE. It is refused whole when the saves need more than the card's
    free space (15 blocks on a PlayStation card, 7,998 kilobytes on a
    formatted PlayStation 2 card), with each save and what it needs listed, or when two of the cards hold different saves
    of one name; a save already on <card> is left as it is. The <from>
    cards are read and never written, so a game's own card stays as it was
    and Per game, chosen again in Graphics Settings, finds it unchanged.

    'new' makes a card under a name, formatted where the medium's module
    can write its format; otherwise the name is the card, and the game
    formats it when it first saves, as a new card. 'dup' copies a whole
    card to a new name and 'rename' renames one; neither needs to read
    the medium's format, so they work for every medium.

THE LOCK
    A running game holds a lock on each card it was given, <card>.lock
    beside the file, for as long as it runs. Every write refuses a card
    that is held, and says by whom: "in use by Amber Saga" for a game of
    this program, "in use by another program" otherwise. Close the game,
    then change its cards; the machine would otherwise write over them.

FORMATS
    A medium's saves are read by its format module, pure functions over
    the card's bytes: the PlayStation's (psx-spx, "Memory Card Data
    Format"), the PlayStation 2's (Ross Ridge's "PlayStation 2 Memory Card
    File System", through emusen-ps2-card; Sapphire_Native.md §30.17) and
    the Sega CD's, its backup RAM and RAM cartridge alike
    (Nephrite_Bram.md). A medium with no module yet (the N64's) lists
    its size, and offers only new, dup and rename.

EXAMPLES
    memcard ls
    cd Saves/PlayStation
    memcard new Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard ls "Amber Saga.mcr"
    memcard cp "Amber Saga.mcr:1" Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard import Cards/Blue.mcr "Amber Saga.mcr" "Basalt Run.mcr"
    memcard ls "../PlayStation 2/Cards/Card 1.ps2"

SEE ALSO
    EmuSen_SaveMedia.md (the model, the modes and the lock),
    Diamond_Cards.md (the PlayStation's cards),
    Sapphire_Native.md §30.17 (the PlayStation 2's cards)
status 0
action none
end

case the page for cheat
target none
covers 1
$ man cheat
out 12273
NAME
    cheat - manage RAM pokes and ROM patches

SYNOPSIS
    cheat add <code> [description]
    cheat poke <space> <addr> <value> [description]
    cheat gg <code> [description]
    cheat rompatch <addr> <value> [<compare>|-] [description]
    cheat list
    cheat master [on|off]
    cheat enable <id>
    cheat disable <id>
    cheat remove <id>
    cheat clear
    cheat save <name>
    cheat load <name>
    cheat files

DESCRIPTION
    Unifies two SNES-specific cheat mechanisms under one command: RAM pokes
    (Pro Action Replay/Game Wizard style) and ROM-read patches (Game Genie
    style). 'add' decodes an 8-character code, guessing which format it is
    from its punctuation (best-effort, not a guarantee - use 'gg' or 'poke'
    directly if the guess is wrong). 'poke'/'rompatch' add a cheat directly
    without code decoding, e.g. for an address already found with 'search'.
    'enable'/'disable' toggle a cheat without removing it.

    Some consoles' codes are wider than a byte or longer than a line - the
    N64 GameShark's 16-bit writes, tests and repeaters. 'add' takes such a
    code whole and makes ONE cheat of all its lines. The shell splits on
    spaces, so join the lines with '+' and leave the space out of each:
    'cheat add D00002000005+810001001234 lives'. A line the console's
    format will not apply is refused with the reason, not stored.

THE MASTER SWITCH
    'cheat master off' silences every cheat at once; 'cheat master on'
    brings them back. It is a SECOND AXIS, not a bulk edit: it does not
    touch any individual cheat's own enabled flag, so switching off to
    check whether a cheat is causing a bug and switching back on returns
    exactly the arrangement you had, however many were on. A cheat added
    while it is off is inert until it is switched back on. 'cheat list'
    says so at the top while it is off, because otherwise every [on] in
    that listing would be a lie.

    It is runtime state and is NOT saved. 'cheat save' does not write it
    and 'cheat load' does not set it: a set saved months ago with the
    switch off would otherwise silently kill every cheat in a session that
    had it on. It starts on, which costs nothing - everything imports
    disabled anyway, so nothing is applying until you say so.

    'save'/'load' persist a named set to /etc/EmuSen/cheats/<name>.json - see
    'man hier'. That file is ordinary JSON with addresses and bytes written
    as hex text, so it can be written or corrected by hand from this shell;
    an entry whose hex doesn't parse is skipped and reported, and the rest of
    the file still loads. 'load' ADDS to whatever is already loaded rather
    than replacing it - run 'cheat clear' first to replace. 'files' lists the
    saved sets. Nothing is loaded automatically: a cheat set applies only
    when you ask for it, so a saved file can't silently alter a later run.

THE WRITE MODEL
    A cheat is one description, one enable flag, and a LIST of writes. That
    matters because real cheats are rarely one address: 'max every item' is
    a run of twenty, and they have to arm and disarm together. One cheat,
    one ID, one toggle, however many addresses it drives - 'cheat list'
    prints a header line and then one indented line per write.

    Each write carries:

        width         1, 2 or 4 bytes. A wide write covers consecutive
                      addresses.
        byte order    little-endian unless the write says otherwise. Only
                      meaningful above one byte.
        type          set, increase or decrease. Increase/decrease read what
                      is there and adjust it, so they accumulate every frame
                      - 'increase by 1' climbs, it does not hold at 1.
                      Or a TEST - if-equal / if-not-equal - which writes
                      nothing: the next write in the cheat that is not a
                      test happens only if it passes, and a run of tests
                      must all pass. 'cheat list' prints one as 'if ... =='.
        bit position  makes the write touch a single bit instead of whole
                      bytes, leaving the other seven alone. For flag bytes
                      where poking the whole byte would clobber unrelated
                      state.
        repeat        count, address stride and value stride. One write can
                      cover a whole run: 8 repetitions stepping the address
                      by 2 pokes every other byte of an inventory table.

    Two combinations are refused rather than half-implemented. A ROM patch
    cannot increase or decrease - the substitution happens at read time and
    the real byte is never written, so there is nothing to accumulate into.
    And a compare byte only works on a single-byte ROM patch, because the
    read hook is handed one byte at a time and cannot check a compare that
    spans several addresses; allowing it would give a torn patch where the
    first byte declines and the rest apply.

IMPORTING RETROARCH CHEATS
    'cheat import <path.cht>' reads RetroArch/libretro .cht files - the
    format the libretro cheat database ships in, one file per game. Both
    shapes a .cht can take are handled:

        cheatN_code       the core-native code string, hex, with several
                          codes joined by '+' becoming one multi-write cheat.
                          Decoded through the same code decoder 'cheat add'
                          uses, so the per-system layout is never hardcoded.
        cheatN_address    RetroArch's own handler fields - cheat_type,
                          memory_search_size, address_bit_position,
                          big_endian, repeat_count and the two strides. These
                          are DECIMAL in a .cht file, unlike the code string.

    memory_search_size 3/4/5 are 1/2/4-byte writes; 0-2 are sub-byte and
    become bit writes at address_bit_position. cheat_type 0 means the entry
    exists but does nothing, and is skipped rather than imported as a no-op.
    An entry that cannot be decoded is skipped and counted; the rest of the
    file still imports.

    Everything imports DISABLED. A database file routinely holds dozens of
    cheats and switching them all on at once is never what anyone meant -
    'cheat list' then 'cheat enable <id>' picks the ones you want.

    'cheat export <path.cht>' writes RAM pokes back out in RetroArch's
    handler form, which round-trips every field above. ROM patches are
    skipped and counted: RetroArch's model has no ROM-read substitution, so
    there is nothing honest to write for them. A cheat containing a test
    is skipped and counted too: one handler entry holds one write, and a
    test with the write it guards dropped would be a different cheat.

THE CHEAT DATABASE
    'cheat db' works over a DIRECTORY TREE of .cht files - one file per
    game, in per-system folders, which is exactly how RetroArch stores its
    cheats. EmuSen ships no cheat data of its own and redistributes none.
    There are two ways to have a database, and neither involves us hosting
    anything:

        Use one you already have. Set AppSettings.CheatDatabaseDirectory to
        an existing RetroArch cheats folder and it is indexed as-is - no
        copying, no conversion. Unset, it defaults to home/Cheats (see
        'man hier').

        'cheat db update' downloads one. It fetches the same archive
        RetroArch's own Online Updater does, straight from libretro to your
        machine, on your explicit request. The data is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0;
        the codes themselves were aggregated from community sources,
        substantially GameHacking.org, and credit belongs to their original
        authors. That notice is printed with the result rather than buried
        here, because attribution is a condition of the licence.

    Only .cht entries are extracted, and an archive entry that tries to
    write outside the target directory is skipped rather than followed.

    'cheat db' with no argument reports where the database is and how many
    files per system. 'find' searches it; 'load' imports the best match,
    disabled, the same way 'import' does. Matching tolerates a loaded ROM's
    file name - 'cheat db load Super Mario World (USA).sfc' finds
    'Super Mario World (USA).cht' - and prefers an exact name over a prefix
    over a substring, so a game whose title is a prefix of another still
    wins for its own name.

    In EmuSen.Mistress the same thing is on the menu bar at Settings > Cheat
    Database..., which shows the folder, what is installed per system, the
    attribution notice, and a download button. Selecting a system there
    lists that system's games (with a filter box - a real SNES folder holds
    several thousand), and picking one loads its cheats into the active
    list, disabled, the same way 'cheat db load' does. It REPLACES that
    list rather than adding to it, unlike the command: picking the same
    game twice from a list is an ordinary thing to do and must not double
    every cheat, whereas typing the command twice is not. An Active
    Cheats... button sits next to Close, since turning a list on is what
    anyone does next.

    Settings > Active Cheats... is the GUI half of 'list', 'enable',
    'disable', 'remove', 'clear' and 'master' - one checkbox per cheat, the
    master switch, and a box to type a code into using the same format
    guess 'add' makes. That list is owned by the frontend rather than by
    the running core, so it survives a Reset and can be built before any
    ROM is loaded; loading a DIFFERENT ROM clears it, since one game's
    addresses mean nothing in another. See EmuSen_Settings_Reference.md
    section 4.14.

    Its Apply Cheats button is not an arm/commit step - ticking a box
    already takes effect on the next frame, the same as 'cheat enable'.
    Apply forces one poke immediately (useful while paused, where no next
    frame is coming) and reports how many landed; it also turns the master
    switch back on, since applying cheats that the switch would swallow is
    not what the button says.

    Save Cheat List writes the list under the running game's file name,
    the same store 'cheat save' uses - so 'cheat load <game>' finds it
    from the shell. Mistress reloads it by itself the next time that game
    starts, which is the point: a saved list is not re-imported from the
    database every session. It never overwrites a list already in hand, so
    a Reset or a close-and-reopen keeps whatever was edited since. See
    section 4.15.

PRUNING THE DATABASE
    The libretro database ships around 44 systems and a build with one
    core can use one of them, so most of a 250MB download is dead weight
    that every database scan still walks. `cheat db prune` deletes the
    system folders no core in this build claims.

    It is core-agnostic: each core declares the cheat-database folder
    names it covers (CoreDescriptor.CheatSystems), and the pruner keeps
    those and drops the rest - so a second core is one registry entry
    rather than an edit to the pruner. Venus claims both the main SNES
    folder and Satellaview, which is the same cartridge hardware.

    Deleting is irreversible and the only way back is another 250MB
    download, so a bare `cheat db prune` only ever lists what would go;
    `--apply` is what actually deletes. It refuses outright rather than
    emptying the database in the two cases that would: when no core
    claims any system at all, and when nothing on disk matches anything
    claimed (a wrong folder, or a wrong mapping). In Mistress the same
    thing is the Prune Unsupported button, whose first click reports and
    whose second deletes; a finished download offers it, since that is
    when the 250MB actually lands. See section 4.16.

EXAMPLES
    cheat poke WRAM 9c 63 infinite lives
    cheat list
    cheat disable 1
    cheat master off
    cheat save zelda
    cheat clear && cheat load zelda
    cheat import "Super Mario World (USA).cht"
    cheat export my-cheats.cht
    cheat db update
    cheat db find mario
    cheat db prune
    cheat db prune --apply
    cheat db load Super Mario World (USA)
status 0
action none
end

case the page for search
target none
covers 1
$ man search
out 1039
NAME
    search - classic 'first scan, then narrow' memory search

SYNOPSIS
    search <space> <value> [<width>]
    search refine <value>
    search changed|unchanged|increased|decreased
    search list [<count>]
    search reset

DESCRIPTION
    Finds where a game stores something without already knowing the address
    (a score, lives, a flag). Start with 'search <space> <value>' to find
    every address currently equal to <value> (width 1/2/4 bytes, default 1,
    little-endian); then narrow with 'refine' (equal to a new value) or
    changed/unchanged/increased/decreased (relative to the last scan/refine)
    until only the real address remains. One search session at a time -
    starting a new 'search <space> ...' replaces whatever was active.
    Refuses to scan a live-hardware-routed space (e.g. CpuBus) outright,
    since bulk-reading it can have real side effects (RDNMI clearing the
    pending-NMI flag, etc.) - use a plain space like WRAM instead.

EXAMPLES
    search WRAM 64
    search refine 65
    search list
status 0
action none
end

case the page for memfind
target none
covers 1
$ man memfind
out 1128
NAME
    memfind - locate a byte sequence in a memory space

SYNOPSIS
    memfind <space> <bytes> [<max>]

DESCRIPTION
    Scans <space> for every offset where <bytes> occurs. Where 'search'
    matches one 1/2/4-byte scalar and then narrows it down over time, this
    matches a whole block in a single pass - the tool for asking where a
    tile's 32 bytes, a decompressed buffer, or a string came from.

    <bytes> is hex byte pairs; ',', ':', '-' and '_' between them are
    ignored, and so is whitespace, so '00FF11', '00 FF 11' and '00:FF:11'
    are the same pattern. '??' in place of a pair matches any byte, which
    is how you skip over the parts of a block that legitimately differ.

    Stops after <max> hits (default 20) and says so, so a pattern that is
    too short to be distinctive reports quickly instead of listing
    thousands of offsets. Same live-hardware-space refusal as 'search' and
    'dump': a byte-by-byte scan of CpuBus could change real emulation
    state, so it is refused outright.

EXAMPLES
    memfind WRAM 00FFFFFFFF00FF00
    memfind VRAM 00??FF??11 5
    memfind ROM 4E696E74656E646F
status 0
action none
end

case the page for snapshot
target none
covers 1
$ man snapshot
out 481
NAME
    snapshot - capture a memory space's full contents

SYNOPSIS
    snapshot <space> <name>
    snapshot list
    snapshot remove <name>

DESCRIPTION
    Saves <space>'s entire current contents under <name> for later 'diff'
    against a further-along state - the general-purpose counterpart to
    'search' when the interesting addresses aren't already narrowed down.
    Same live-hardware-space refusal as 'search'/'dump'.

EXAMPLES
    snapshot WRAM before
    diff before
status 0
action none
end

case the page for diff
target none
covers 1
$ man diff
out 439
NAME
    diff - compare a snapshot against current memory

SYNOPSIS
    diff <name> [<count>]

DESCRIPTION
    Compares a snapshot saved with 'snapshot' against that same space's
    CURRENT contents and lists every byte address that's different now
    (default 20 shown). Doesn't touch or replace the saved snapshot, so the
    same baseline can be diffed again later against a further state.

EXAMPLES
    diff before
    diff before 50
status 0
action none
end

case the page for dump
target none
covers 1
$ man dump
out 903
NAME
    dump - write a raw memory range to disk

SYNOPSIS
    dump <space> <addr> <len> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Writes <len> raw bytes from <space> starting at <addr> to
    home/Logs/<CoreName>/<file> - no header or
    metadata, so a hex editor can open the result directly. The write-side
    counterpart is 'load'. Same live-hardware-space refusal as
    'search'/'snapshot'.

    The file name is taken from home/Logs/<CoreName>/: a bare name or one
    with '../' in it is resolved from there, one with a leading '/' from
    this shell's root, as every other command reads a path (see 'man
    hier'). A name that lands above the root is refused, so '../../../x'
    and an absolute path of the machine cannot write or read outside it.
    Before 2026-10-10 the name was joined to the folder unchecked, and
    both did.

EXAMPLES
    dump WRAM 0 2000 wram.bin
    dump WRAM 0 2000 /tmp/wram.bin
status 0
action none
end

