end

case the page for disasm
target none
covers 1
$ man disasm
out 2992
NAME
    disasm - disassemble instructions

SYNOPSIS
    disasm <space> <addr> [<n>]
    disasm <cpu> [<addr>] [<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Disassembles <n> instructions (default 10) starting at <addr>, printing
    address, raw bytes, mnemonic, and operand for each. A linear
    disassembler has no way to know which bytes are really code vs. data
    mixed into the same range, so a run through embedded data can produce
    garbage until it happens to resync - a known, accepted limitation, not a
    bug.

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A chip name in place of a space disassembles that chip's own code, in
    its own instruction set - see 'man cpus'. The SNES needs four different
    disassemblers to cover itself: the 65816 for the main CPU and the SA-1,
    the SPC700 for sound, the GSU's RISC encoding, and the NEC DSP's fixed
    24-bit words. Naming the chip picks the right one; naming a raw space
    picks by space, which is why 'disasm APURAM' decodes SPC700 and not
    65816 - it used to decode 65816, and quietly produced nonsense.

    With no address, disassembly starts wherever that chip is executing
    right now, which is usually what you want at a breakpoint.

    One asymmetry worth knowing: 'disasm DSPPRG' indexes program WORDS, not
    bytes, because the NEC DSP's program counter is a word index and that
    is the only number you ever have to paste in. Reading DSPPRG through
    'mem' is still byte-addressed, three bytes per word.

OPERAND WIDTHS (65816 ONLY)
    On the 65816 an immediate operand is one byte or two depending on the
    M (accumulator) and X (index) flags, which are not in the instruction
    bytes. By default those come from the CPU's flags RIGHT NOW, and
    REP/SEP inside the range are then tracked forward - which is correct
    when disassembling from the current PC, and only a guess anywhere
    else. Guessing wrong shifts every following instruction by a byte, so
    the listing stays syntactically plausible while being entirely wrong:
    a routine that really runs 8-bit-index reads as 'LDY #$C500' where the
    bytes are 'LDY #$00' followed by the start of the next instruction.

    'm8'/'m16' and 'x8'/'x16' force the starting widths. They may appear
    in any order after the command name and don't count as <addr>/<n>.
    The chosen widths are echoed above the listing. Asking for a 16-bit
    width also selects native mode, because emulation mode forces both
    widths to 8 regardless of M/X - without that, 'x16' would silently do
    nothing whenever the CPU happens to be paused in emulation mode.

    If a disassembly looks like it decodes into nonsense a few
    instructions in - implausible operands, a store to a read-only
    register, addresses that don't line up with a known write site - try
    the other widths before concluding the bytes are data.

EXAMPLES
    disasm CpuBus 8000 20
    disasm gsu
    disasm spc 05A5 10
    disasm dsp 0 20
    disasm cpu 04FDD8 12 m16 x8

SEE ALSO
    cpus, cov, bt, label
status 0
action none
end

case the page for trace
target none
covers 1
$ man trace
out 692
NAME
    trace - arm a live CPU instruction trace

SYNOPSIS
    trace <count>
    trace off

DESCRIPTION
    Arms this core's own live CPU instruction trace for the next <count>
    instructions, counted from whenever this command runs (not from
    power-on) - so a trace can be aimed at a specific moment in a play
    session instead of burning its whole budget during boot. 'trace off'
    cancels an in-progress trace early. <count> is at least 1: a trace of
    no instructions is refused, since a core's trace armed with 0 never ends.
    Not available for a core with no equivalent trace mechanism, or a
    standalone launch with no core loaded.

EXAMPLES
    trace 500
    trace off
status 0
action none
end

case the page for callers
target none
covers 1
$ man callers
out 547
NAME
    callers - find static call/jump references to an address

SYNOPSIS
    callers <addr> [<scanstart> <scanlen>]

DESCRIPTION
    Scans CpuBus (default: <addr>'s own bank, $8000-$FFFF) for instructions
    statically calling/jumping to <addr> - a static-analysis alternative to
    setting a breakpoint and waiting for it to hit: finds every place that
    COULD jump there, whether or not it was ever actually reached during a
    given play session. See 'writers'/'readers' for the memory-access
    equivalents.

EXAMPLES
    callers 8000
status 0
action none
end

case the page for writers
target none
covers 1
$ man writers
out 650
NAME
    writers - find static write references to an address

SYNOPSIS
    writers <addr> [<scanstart> <scanlen>]

DESCRIPTION
    Scans CpuBus (default: <addr>'s own bank, $8000-$FFFF) for instructions
    statically storing to <addr> - answers 'what code exists that's capable
    of writing here', regardless of whether that write path was ever
    actually reached during a traced run (which is all a live 'watch' can
    tell you). Only finds statically-known addressing modes (absolute/
    absolute-long); indexed/indirect writes aren't visible to static
    analysis and won't show up here - use 'watch' for those.

EXAMPLES
    writers 13c6
status 0
action none
end

