case the page for sed
target none
covers 1
$ man sed
out 929
NAME
    sed - stream substitution

SYNOPSIS
    sed s/pattern/replacement/[gi]
    sed <expr> <text...>

DESCRIPTION
    A basic sed-style filter supporting only the s/// substitution command
    (no line addressing, no d/p/hold-space/etc). The delimiter can be any
    character, matching real sed's own 's#/bin#/usr/bin#' convention, with
    '\<delim>' escaping a literal delimiter inside the pattern/replacement.
    Pattern is a .NET regex. Without 'g', only the first match on EACH LINE
    is replaced (not just the first match in the whole piped blob) - most
    output this runs against (regs, watch log, ...) is multi-line, so this
    matters. Replacement supports '&' (whole match) and \1-\9 (capture
    groups), same as real sed. Real use is as a pipeline stage; standalone
    'sed <expr> <text...>' works too when there's nothing to pipe from.

EXAMPLES
    regs | sed s/PC=/pc=/
    echo hello | sed s/hello/hi/
status 0
action none
end

case the page for grep
target none
covers 1
$ man grep
out 830
NAME
    grep - filter lines by pattern

SYNOPSIS
    grep [-i] [-v] [-n] [-c] [-q] <pattern> [text...]

DESCRIPTION
    Filters lines matching <pattern> (a .NET regex, same choice sed makes).
    -i case-insensitive, -v invert (show non-matching lines), -n prefix each
    match with its 1-based line number, -c print only the match count, -q
    no output at all (exit code only). Exit code is 0 if anything matched, 1
    otherwise (real grep semantics) - -q exists specifically so grep can
    drive an if/while condition ('if regs | grep -q PC; then ...'), not just
    filter text. Reads piped stdin if present, else operates on trailing
    literal text. No -E/-F/-P mode switches, no -A/-B/-C context lines, no
    multi-file support.

EXAMPLES
    watch log 1 | grep -v poll
    if regs | grep -q PC; then echo has-pc; fi
status 0
action none
end

