case the page for cd
target none
covers 1
$ man cd
out 1179
NAME
    cd - change the current directory

SYNOPSIS
    cd [dir]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes the process-wide current directory - the same one 'pwd' reads
    and every relative path elsewhere in this shell (redirection, 'ls', 'mv',
    'rm', 'source', 'wc <path>', ...) resolves against. No argument goes to
    the current account's own home directory (see 'whoami'/'hier') - real
    $HOME semantics, scoped to this shell's own tree. A path starting with
    '/' means THIS shell's own root, not the real OS filesystem root -
    'cd /SourceLogs' works from anywhere, the same way a real chroot makes '/'
    mean the chroot directory (see 'hier'). Walled to the project root no
    matter how it's spelled - no amount of 'cd ..', a leading '/', or a long
    '../../..' chain can leave it; this is a deliberate walled garden against
    accidents, not a security boundary. A directory name with spaces doesn't
    need quoting ('cd My Folder' works) - cd only ever takes one path, so
    everything after it is treated as that path literally; quoting
    ('cd "My Folder"') also works if you prefer it.

EXAMPLES
    cd EmuSen.DianaSh
    cd /SourceLogs
    cd My Folder
    cd
status 0
action none
end

case the page for hier
target none
covers 1
$ man hier
out 9370
NAME
    hier - this shell's own filesystem layout

SYNOPSIS
    man hier

DESCRIPTION
    A real Unix precedent for this exact page: 'man hier' documents a
    filesystem's layout without 'hier' being a runnable command - same here.

    /                 your home, and the whole of what this shell can see.
                      'cd' with no argument comes back here. The install
                      itself - the binaries, the repo when running from
                      source - sits ABOVE this root and is unreachable.

    /Games            your ROM library
    /Saves            battery-backed cartridge SRAM ('.srm'), a folder per
                      console: NES, SNES, N64, GB, GBC
    /Saves/Save States   'state save'/'state load' snapshots
    /Library          Mistress's own databases, written and read by the
                      program, not by hand: games.db (favourites, play
                      time, collections, edits) and records.db (who wrote
                      each save state, and the shader pack's build date).
                      Save states no longer get a .json file beside them;
                      old ones are read into records.db and left in place
    /Shaders          shader packs downloaded on request
    /Firmware         firmware files you supply. A folder of your own, set
                      as FirmwareDirectory on Preferences > Firmware, is
                      searched first and only ever read
    /Cheats           your own .cht tree - see 'man cheat'
    /Cores            core libraries you add yourself. Each is loaded only
                      once you approve it, by its SHA-256; a changed file
                      is asked about again. The approvals are kept in
                      /etc/EmuSen/cores.approved, one 'sha256sum' line
                      each. See EmuSen_Backend.md section 29.40
    /Logs             'dump'/'load'/screenshot/recording output
    /etc/EmuSen       every config file the emulator keeps: appsettings.json,
                      keybindings.json, gamepadbindings.json,
                      hotkeybindings.json, audio.json, graphics.json,
                      cores.approved (the cores you approved, see /Cores), and
                      cheats/<name>.json, and themes/<name>.axaml or
                      themes/<name>.css (either a ResourceDictionary or a
                      restricted CSS, both overriding EmuSen.LunaP's Luna*
                      keys - see EmuSen_LunaP.md). Plain JSON, meant to be read and
                      edited from this shell - comments and trailing commas
                      are tolerated. Written by EmuSen.Galaxia; these used to
                      live outside the sandbox under the OS's own per-user
                      config directory, and are copied in the first time each
                      file is read (the originals are left where they were)
    /bin              published builds only: one launcher per frontend, each
                      a two-line shim onto the real binary in /lib/EmuSen
    /lib/EmuSen       published builds only: the whole .NET application
                      directory - every shipped .dll, the apphosts,
                      deps.json/runtimeconfig.json, runtimes/. The Unix
                      '/usr/lib/<app>' shape: private libraries of one app,
                      not a shared library dir. Absent from source
    /tmp              scratch space, nothing here is ever auto-deleted.
                      '/tmp/WiseMan' is the test suite's own scratch

    /Documents        published builds only: the EmuSen Manual and the
                      per-console hardware notes under 'Man pages/', staged
                      in at publish time, or on a Mac copied in from
                      EmuSen.app. Running from source they stay in
                      the repo where they are edited, which is above this
                      root - use 'man' rather than 'cat' from source

    Every directory above is located by EmuSen.Galaxia, not by this shell -
    the sandbox forwards to it, so a core never asks a debugger where a save
    goes. See EmuSen_Galaxia.md.

    '/home' used to be five levels down, at 'EmuSen.DianaSh/DianaSh/Usr/
    Home' - a published user's save folder named after a C# project. If you
    have an install from before the move, everything the emulator wrote
    (saves, states, firmware, cheats, logs) was COPIED to '/home' on first
    run and the originals left untouched. Your ROMs were NOT copied - a
    library is too big to duplicate behind your back. They are still in
    'EmuSen.DianaSh/DianaSh/Usr/Home/{Games,Roms}'; move them into
    '/home/Games' when convenient, or just point RomDirectory at them in
    Preferences, which is where the library is configured anyway.

    A leading '/' means THIS root - your home - not the real OS filesystem
    root, and not the install directory. Every command that names a file
    ('cat', 'ls', 'find', 'awk', 'nano', redirection, 'source', 'dump',
    'load', 'cov save|load', 'label', 'cheat import|export', 'core',
    'state', 'test -f|-d') resolves through the same check and refuses
    anything above it, so no amount of '../..' walks out. Two paths are
    allowed outside the root on purpose: 'core' reads a ROM by an absolute
    path inside the library configured as RomDirectory, and 'state' with
    no path uses the frontend's own save-state path. The check is on the
    path's text and does not follow a symbolic link: a link inside the
    root that points out of it is followed by whatever opens it. Before
    2026-10-10, 'dump', 'load', 'cov', 'core', 'state' and 'test' did
    not go through the check at all.

    This shell used to be rooted at the project directory, which meant
    'ls /' listed '.git', 'EmuSen.sln' and every C# project beside your
    saves; rooting at home is what stopped that. It is a guardrail against
    accidents, not a security boundary.

WHERE THE ROOT ACTUALLY IS
    Decided once at startup by walking up from the running assembly's own
    folder (not the current directory, which a launcher or shortcut gets
    to choose and so can't be trusted), stopping at the first parent that
    holds either marker:

    'EmuSen.sln'          running from source: the project root, as
                          described above. A build output dir
                          ('bin/Release/net10.0') is three levels down, so
                          Debug and Release both land on the same root and
                          share one set of saves and logs.

    '.dianashroot'        a published build: the publish directory itself,
                          written there at publish time. No 'EmuSen.sln'
                          ships with a published app, so this is what the
                          walk finds instead - two levels up from
                          '/lib/EmuSen', where the binaries live.

    A tree published before the shell was renamed carries '.dianaosroot',
    which is found the same way.

    If neither turns up, the fallback is 'DianaShRoot/' beside the binary
    (or an existing 'DianaOSRoot/' there, when no 'DianaShRoot/' is).
    That is what a publish looks like WITHOUT the layout below - the doc
    trees stage into 'DianaShRoot/' either way, so an app dir copied out by
    hand still finds its own manual instead of coming up rootless.

    On macOS, EmuSen installs as 'EmuSen.app', which is read-only, so the
    root is '~/Library/Application Support/EmuSen' instead. It is taken
    BEFORE the walk when the program runs from inside a bundle
    ('EmuSen.app/Contents/MacOS'), and as the fallback when the walk finds
    neither marker. From source, or from a marked tree, a Mac roots where
    Linux does. '/Documents' is copied out of the bundle on every start,
    never over a file already there. See EmuSen_Galaxia.md §3.4.

    A published tree puts the binaries in 'lib/EmuSen', the launchers in
    'bin', and the shell's root at 'home' BESIDE them - so every shipped
    .dll and the running executable are now ABOVE this shell's root and
    cannot be reached from it at all.

    That was not always true. When the shell was rooted at the install
    directory, real '/' contained real '/lib', and 'rm' and 'mv' were
    suspended precisely because nothing here distinguished '/lib/EmuSen'
    from your own files. Rooting at home removed the exposure those two
    suspensions existed for; they are still suspended, which is now a
    stricter setting than the layout requires rather than a necessity.

    A published root is NOT empty. Both read-only doc trees stage into
    '/Documents' at publish time, so 'man', 'cat', 'find' and 'grep' have
    the same reference material there as they do from source. Everything
    else - the six directories under '/home' - is created empty on first
    run.

    The layout is MSBuild's doing, not this shell's: see
    EmuSen.DianaSh/Publish/DianaShPublishLayout.targets, imported by each
    publishable frontend. It only rewrites where files are copied - the
    .NET application directory is relocated whole, never taken apart, so
    nothing about how the app resolves its assemblies changes. Publish
    with -p:DianaShPublishLayout=false for a plain flat app dir.

EXAMPLES
    man hier
    ls /
    cd /home/Logs && ls
status 0
action none
end

case the page for dianash
target none
covers 1
$ man dianash
out 1609
NAME
    dianash - the standalone DianaSh shell, with no emulator in it

SYNOPSIS
    dianash
    dianash -c <line>...

DESCRIPTION
    The program a terminal runs to get DianaSh on its own: the shell's
    language and builtins, its sessions and accounts, and the sandbox
    rooted at the tree's home, in one file, with a line editor. It knows
    no core and provides no service, so a debugger command answers there
    as it does with no ROM loaded; a frontend's console is the place to
    reach a running game.

    With no argument it prints its banner and reads lines at a prompt,
    'DianaSh #: ' (with the session's name once 'tmux new' has made a
    second), or '> ' while a block, a quote or a $(...) is still open.
    The editor is the C# shell's: Up and Down walk the history, Left,
    Right, Home and End move along the line, Backspace and Delete remove a
    character, and Escape clears the line. Ctrl-C discards the line being
    typed, and stops a line that is running at its next check; Ctrl-D on
    an empty line ends the shell, as 'shutdown' does. When standard input
    is not a terminal, lines are read as they come and nothing is echoed.

    With -c, each argument is run as one line typed at the prompt, in
    order, and the program exits with the last line's $?.

    The shell starts in its home, the '/' every path is resolved against
    (see 'man hier'), creating it if it is not there. Its manual pages are
    those beside the program, in dianash/man.

EXAMPLES
    dianash
    dianash -c 'echo hello | cat'
    dianash -c 'for i in 1 2 3; do echo $i; done' 'echo $?'
status 0
action none
end

case the page for which
target none
covers 1
$ man which
out 812
NAME
    which - say what answers a name

SYNOPSIS
    which <name>...

DESCRIPTION
    For each name, one line saying what runs when it is typed: 'shell
    builtin' for a name the shell answers itself (the language's, such as
    'source' and 'man', and the shell's own commands, such as 'cd' and
    'tmux'), 'alias for <command>' for one of the shell's aliases, and
    'command' for any other command it has. A name nothing answers prints
    'which: no <name>', and makes the exit status 1. Case is ignored, as
    it is when a command is run.

    A host's command that takes the place of a shell builtin of the same
    name is a builtin to the C# shell and a command to the Rust one; that
    is the one place the two answer differently.

EXAMPLES
    which cd grep quit
    which nosuch || echo missing
status 0
action none
end

case the page for command
target none
covers 1
$ man command
out 657
NAME
    command - run a command as itself, or say whether it exists

SYNOPSIS
    command <name> [args...]
    command -v <name>...

DESCRIPTION
    'command <name>' runs <name> with its arguments without mapping it
    through the shell's aliases: 'command quit' is refused as an unknown
    command where 'quit' alone shuts the shell down. The language's own
    names still mean themselves.

    'command -v' prints each name that would run, one to a line, and
    nothing for one that would not; the exit status is 1 when any would
    not, so it serves as a test in an 'if'.

EXAMPLES
    command echo hello
    if command -v grep; then echo found; fi
status 0
action none
end

case the page for trust
target none
covers 1
$ man trust
out 862
NAME
    trust - approve a module file you added, by its hash

SYNOPSIS
    trust <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Records a file's SHA-256 hash as approved, so that a command module
    you added to the shell can be loaded once loading one is supported:
    a module runs inside the program with your full permissions, and is
    approved once, by you, at the prompt. A changed file has another hash
    and must be approved again.

    The approval is given only at the prompt: 'trust' refuses inside a
    script run with 'source' and inside $(...), so a script cannot approve
    a file on your behalf. The path is resolved through the sandbox, as
    every path is (see 'man hier'). Approvals are kept, one hash and path
    to a line, in the 'trusted' file of the 'dianash' folder beside the
    program's configuration.

EXAMPLES
    trust /lib/dianash/mymodule.so
status 0
action none
end

case the page for theme
target none
covers 1
$ man theme
out 5261
NAME
    theme - the colours and fonts every EmuSen window is drawn with

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/EmuSen/themes/<name>.css
    /etc/EmuSen/themes/<name>.axaml

DESCRIPTION
    A theme overrides whichever of the Luna* palette keys it cares about
    and leaves the rest alone, so a two-line theme is a legitimate theme.
    Drop a file in the directory above and pick it in Preferences; there is
    no registration step and no restart. One name is one theme: if both
    formats spell it, the .axaml is the one that loads.

    The .css form is a restricted CSS - a format, not an engine. There is
    no cascade, no specificity, no inheritance and no box model, and the
    only things it can produce are colours, numbers, font families and the
    property setters listed below. The .axaml form is an Avalonia
    ResourceDictionary and is documented in EmuSen_LunaP.md section 12.

THE PALETTE
    Written inside ':root'. Each token is a Luna* resource key in kebab
    case, and setting a colour sets both halves the palette spells (the
    Color and the brush), so nothing half-applies.

    --luna-surface           tool-window background
    --luna-input-surface     text-input background
    --luna-void              letterbox area behind a game frame
    --luna-border            card edges, panel rules, splitter dividers
    --luna-text              body and monospace text
    --luna-meter-text        meter-row labels and values
    --luna-muted             hints, group headers, disabled captions
    --luna-section-header    section headings
    --luna-accent            selection, focus and the filled half of a
                             stock control - the first token in this
                             palette for something LunaP does not draw
    --luna-on-accent         whatever is drawn ON the accent: a tick, a
                             label inside a selected row
    --luna-warning           inline caution text
    --luna-error             a failure, a refusal, an invalid field
    --luna-success           a thing that worked
    --luna-info              a thing in progress, or worth noticing
    --luna-nominal           load ramp, below 60%
    --luna-busy              load ramp, 60% and above
    --luna-hot               load ramp, 85% and above
    --luna-hud-surface       the bar and the notices over a running game
    --luna-mono-font         the monospace family list
    --luna-hint-font-size    hint text size
    --luna-header-font-size  section heading size

    The key's SUFFIX decides how its value is read: '...-size' is a number,
    '...-font' a font family, everything else a colour. A font family and a
    named colour are written identically ('monospace', 'gainsboro'), so the
    value alone could not settle it.

    Colours take any CSS spelling - #RGB, #RRGGBB, #RGBA, #RRGGBBAA,
    rgb(), rgba() with alpha 0-1, and the named colours. In the eight-digit
    form ALPHA IS LAST, the CSS order; Avalonia's own #AARRGGBB reads the
    same digits backwards, and inside a .css file CSS wins.

RULES
    Outside ':root', a block styles one kind of control. The selector is
    'element', 'element.state' or 'element part', and the element is the
    control's own name in kebab case:

    button-bar        card              console-pane      dropdown
    empty-state       error-text        field-row         filter-bar
    hint-text         luna-switch       menu-bar          meter-list
    meter-row         mono-text         path-picker-row   rgba-image-view
    section-header    side-panel        split-pane        status-bar
    tabs              tool-bar

    States: meter-row.nominal, meter-row.busy, meter-row.hot.
    Parts:  meter-row .bar; filter-bar .search, .facet;
            console-pane .output, .input, .prompt;
            empty-state .message, .detail;
            card .header, .content; split-pane .rule;
            side-panel .title, .close, .content.

    Properties: background, background-color, color, font-family,
    font-size, font-weight. A value may be a token - 'var(--luna-hot)' -
    which FOLLOWS that token rather than copying it; a rule that restates a
    colour stops tracking the palette.

WHEN IT DOES NOT LOAD
    Two tiers, on purpose. A SYNTAX error refuses the whole file and leaves
    the previous theme in force: an unbalanced brace, a declaration with no
    colon, an unterminated comment, an at-rule, a nested rule. An UNKNOWN
    selector, state, part, property or unreadable value is reported and
    skipped, and the rest of the theme still applies - so a theme written
    for a later EmuSen keeps working here.

    Either way the reason is printed the same way a config file's is, with
    the line number from your file: comments are not stripped before
    counting, so the number matches what your editor shows.

    A theme that is deleted or broken between runs falls back to built-in
    WITHOUT forgetting the choice. Fix the file, restart, and it returns.

EXAMPLES
    cat > /etc/EmuSen/themes/Nocturne.css
    :root {
      --luna-surface:        #12131A;
      --luna-section-header: #7AA2F7;
      --luna-mono-font:      "Fira Code", monospace;
    }
    meter-row.hot .bar { color: var(--luna-hot); }

    ls /etc/EmuSen/themes
status 0
action none
end

