case the page for xxd
target none
covers 1
$ man xxd
out 1080
NAME
    xxd, hexdump - hexdump a real file's raw bytes

SYNOPSIS
    xxd <path>
    hexdump <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Reads <path>'s REAL bytes off disk (not decoded as text the way
    'cat'/'head'/'tail' do, which would corrupt anything that isn't valid
    UTF8) and prints them in the same address/hex/ASCII layout 'mem' uses
    for a live memory space - the on-disk counterpart to 'mem', purpose-
    built so a 'dump'd capture can be inspected without leaving the shell
    for an external hex editor. Walled to the project's own directory tree
    (see 'cd'). With no <path>, hexdumps piped stdin instead - UTF8-encoded
    first, since a pipeline stage's own output is always already-decoded
    text here, never raw bytes (this shell's pipes have no byte-stream
    concept the way a real Unix pipe does). 'hexdump' is a plain alias, not
    a separate command - and NOT real hexdump's own multi-format output,
    just this same one layout under a second, equally-reached-for name.

EXAMPLES
    dump WRAM 0 256 wram.bin
    xxd home/Logs/SNES/wram.bin
    echo hi | xxd
status 0
action none
end

case the page for nano
target none
covers 1
$ man nano
out 1090
NAME
    nano - simple full-screen text editor

SYNOPSIS
    nano <path>

DESCRIPTION
    A small full-screen editor for creating/editing a real file (a 'source'
    script, say) without leaving the shell for an external editor. NOT a
    full GNU nano clone - no search, no cut/paste ring, no syntax
    highlighting - just arrow-key movement, insert/Backspace/Delete, and
    Ctrl+O to save / Ctrl+X to exit (prompting to save first if there are
    unsaved changes). <path> is created if it doesn't exist yet, and is
    walled to the project's own directory tree like every other real-file
    command here (see 'cd'). Needs a REAL interactive terminal - it takes
    over the console with raw key reads and full-screen redraws the same
    way this shell's own line editor does for a single line, just for a
    whole buffer - so it refuses cleanly (rather than failing strangely) when
    stdin/stdout is redirected (a piped script, a 'source'd file, a headless
    test) or when there's no real terminal at all (EmuSen.Mistress's GUI
    console window).

EXAMPLES
    nano setup.txt
status 0
action none
end

