action none
end

case the page for coretop
target none
covers 1
$ man coretop
out 3129
NAME
    coretop - live htop-style dashboard of the loaded core's hardware

SYNOPSIS
    coretop [-w]

DESCRIPTION
    A live, auto-refreshing (4x/second) dashboard, styled after htop: per-
    subsystem hardware load bars, CPU registers, a sprite-capacity gauge,
    per-voice audio channel meters, a live color-RAM palette swatch, and (if
    this core has a tilemap concept) a downsampled live preview of VRAM's
    tile sheet - all in one screen, refreshing on its own clock rather than
    waiting for input. Every number comes from IDebugTarget, so a core with
    nothing to report for a given section (no hardware-load breakdown wired
    up, no fixed sprite capacity, no palettes) just makes that section
    shrink or disappear rather than showing something fake.

    On a core of the stable ABI the load bars are the phases the core times
    and names in its machine info; a core that times none shows one bar,
    'frame', the engine's own timing of the whole frame. Each is a share of
    one frame at the machine's own rate.

    Load bars are colored green/yellow/red under 60% / 60-85% / over 85%,
    the same rough 'getting busy' convention real htop's CPU bars use.

    Press Ctrl+C to exit - unlike everywhere else in this shell, Ctrl+C is
    read as a key here (via Console.TreatControlCAsInput) rather than
    raising a process-level interrupt, restored to normal the moment
    coretop exits. Needs a REAL interactive terminal, same as 'nano' and
    for the same reason - refuses cleanly rather than trying to draw
    anywhere when stdin/stdout is redirected or there's no real terminal at
    all (a piped script, a 'source'd file, a headless test).

    In EmuSen.Mistress's own console window (a TextBox, not a real
    terminal - the above wouldn't work there at all), 'coretop' is replaced
    with a windowed equivalent instead: a real, non-blocking Avalonia window
    showing the same data as actual widgets and live images (the palette and
    VRAM tile sheet render as real pictures there, not ANSI blocks) that
    refreshes on its own timer while gameplay keeps running - Ctrl+C doesn't
    apply there, just close the window.

    -w  Open the same dashboard in a separate window instead of taking over
        the terminal, so you can dismiss it and keep playing. In
        EmuSen.Mistress, coretop is already windowed by default, so -w is
        accepted and simply has no additional effect there. In EmuSen.Hotaru
        (a Raylib console build with no window of its own to reuse), -w spins
        up a small dedicated Avalonia UI thread the first time it's used and
        opens a real second OS window from it, entirely separate from the
        Raylib game window and its main loop - gameplay keeps running behind
        it. Closing that window is -w's equivalent of Ctrl+C: it's the whole
        'stop watching coretop' action, nothing else to press. If the current
        frontend has no window to open at all, -w fails cleanly with
        "not supported by this frontend" rather than falling back to the
        terminal dashboard.

EXAMPLES
    coretop
    coretop -w
status 0
action none
end

case the page for vstop
target none
covers 1
$ man vstop
out 6186
NAME
    vstop - live htop-style dashboard of the .NET runtime this process runs on

SYNOPSIS
    vstop [-w]

DESCRIPTION
    The counterpart to 'coretop'. Where that one watches the emulated
    machine, this one watches the real one underneath it: the .NET virtual
    machine that DianaSh, the loaded core, and the frontend are all running
    on. Managed heap, garbage collector, thread pool, process memory and
    CPU, refreshing twice a second until you quit.

    It is the one dashboard that still has something to show with NO ROM
    loaded, because it reports on the host VM rather than a guest - it
    takes no IDebugTarget at all and is completely core-agnostic.

    Four bars across the top, colored green/yellow/red under 60% / 60-85% /
    over 85% the same way coretop's are:

        CPU        this process's processor time, divided by core count, so
                   100% means every core busy - htop's aggregate scale, not
                   the per-core one where an 8-core box can read 800%.
        Machine    system-wide memory in use. This is the figure the GC
                   compares against its own high-load threshold when it
                   decides to collect more aggressively, so it explains GC
                   behaviour that the heap numbers alone do not.
        Heap frag  fragmented bytes over committed bytes - dead space inside
                   the managed heap that the GC has not handed back. A high
                   reading next to a small live heap is the signature of a
                   pinning or large-object problem.
        Pool load  thread-pool threads against the pool maximum. Climbing
                   toward the ceiling with a non-zero queued-work count is
                   thread-pool starvation.

    Below them: process working set and private bytes; managed heap, GC-
    committed bytes, cumulative allocations and the live allocation rate;
    per-generation sizes (gen0/gen1/gen2, then LOH and POH where the
    runtime reports them); GC mode, latency mode, pause-time percentage,
    per-generation collection counts with a collections-per-minute rate and
    total time paused; OS thread and handle counts, pool thread counts and
    limits, queued and completed work items with a throughput rate; and the
    number of loaded assemblies.

    Allocation rate, CPU percent, collections per minute and work-item
    throughput are all rates, so they need two readings to exist. The first
    reading is taken before the first frame is drawn - you never see a
    screen of zeroes - but they stay zero for a single refresh if the
    dashboard is somehow drawn without that priming sample.

    A subtler point, and the reason several fields can read '-' instead of
    a number: GC.GetGCMemoryInfo() does not describe the heap right now, it
    describes the heap AS OF THE LAST COLLECTION. In a process that has not
    collected yet - which a freshly launched shell has not - every figure
    derived from it is legitimately zero: committed bytes, fragmented bytes,
    machine memory load, per-generation sizes, pause-time percentage. Those
    are exactly the readings someone opening this dashboard is most likely
    to act on, and a bar sitting confidently at 0.0% is worse than no bar,
    because it looks like an answer. So the Machine and Heap frag bars draw
    empty with '(no GC yet - press G)' until a collection has actually
    happened, and the committed/per-generation/pause-time fields show '-'.
    Press G once and the whole screen fills in. Working set, private bytes,
    managed heap, cumulative allocations, thread counts and CPU are all
    live readings and never do this.

    Nothing this command reads is allowed to throw. Every counter is read
    behind a guard that falls back to zero, because a runtime or platform
    that does not expose one of them (handle counts are not meaningful
    everywhere) must degrade to a missing number, not take the shell down.

    KEYS
        Ctrl+C, Q   exit. As in coretop, Ctrl+C is read as a key here (via
                    Console.TreatControlCAsInput) rather than raising a
                    process-level interrupt, and is restored the moment
                    vstop exits.
        G           force a full blocking GC.Collect(). Deliberately
                    included: the most common question this dashboard gets
                    opened to answer is 'is that a leak or just uncollected
                    garbage', and collecting on demand answers it in one
                    keystroke. It is a diagnostic, not something to lean on.

    Needs a REAL interactive terminal, same as 'nano' and 'coretop' and for
    the same reason - refuses cleanly rather than drawing anywhere when
    stdin/stdout is redirected or there is no terminal at all (a piped
    script, a 'source'd file, a headless test).

    In EmuSen.Mistress's own console window (a TextBox, not a real
    terminal - the above wouldn't work there at all), 'vstop' is replaced
    with a windowed equivalent, exactly as 'coretop' is: a real, non-
    blocking Avalonia window showing the same figures as widgets, with the
    four meters as real progress bars on the same green/yellow/red
    thresholds, refreshing on its own timer while gameplay keeps running.
    Ctrl+C, Q and G don't apply there - close the window to dismiss it, and
    use its 'Collect now' button for what G does here. The same window is
    on the menu bar at Settings > Runtime Dashboard..., which unlike
    Hardware Dashboard beside it is never greyed out, because this one has
    something to show with no ROM loaded.

    -w  Open the same dashboard in a separate window instead of taking over
        the terminal. Wired per frontend; where nothing is wired up it fails
        cleanly with "not supported by this frontend" rather than falling
        back to the terminal dashboard. Unlike coretop's -w, this one passes
        no debug target, because there is nothing here that depends on a
        loaded core. In EmuSen.Mistress vstop is already windowed, so -w is
        accepted and simply has no additional effect.

EXAMPLES
    vstop
    vstop -w
status 0
action none
end

case the page for feed
target none
covers 1
$ man feed
out 1808
NAME
    feed - resume the game and keep watching it

SYNOPSIS
    feed [-w]

DESCRIPTION
    Resumes gameplay, like 'resume', but leaves a way back to the prompt
    that does not depend on the game window having keyboard focus: press
    Ctrl+C at the TERMINAL and the prompt reopens.

    That watch is a per-frame poll of Console.KeyAvailable, not a blocking
    read - the same non-blocking technique coretop's dashboard uses, spread
    across frames because gameplay has to keep running underneath it. While
    armed, Console.TreatControlCAsInput is set, so Ctrl+C arrives as a
    readable key rather than the process-terminating signal it is
    everywhere else. Entering the prompt by ANY path disarms it, so a plain
    Ctrl+C means 'shut down' again the moment you are back at a prompt.

    -w  Also opens the live picture in a separate window, so gameplay is
        visible without the game window itself being on screen. Refreshed
        at ~30Hz into one reused bitmap. Same accepted read-only race as
        coretop -w: the timer reads core state off the UI thread with no
        synchronization against the emulation thread's own RunFrame.

    In EmuSen.Mistress the word means the same thing but does much less,
    and deliberately so. That frontend's game view is already on screen
    continuously, in the same window, the whole time its console window is
    open - the two are just independent Avalonia windows - so there is no
    separate feed to open. 'feed' there brings the game window to the
    front, for when the console (or something else) is covering it, and
    ignores '-w' the same way that frontend's own 'coretop' does. The
    command exists there for consistency: one word means 'let me see the
    game' everywhere DianaSh runs.

EXAMPLES
    feed
    feed -w
status 0
action none
end

case the page for mv
target none
covers 1
$ man mv
out 916
NAME
    mv - move or rename a file or directory

SYNOPSIS
    mv <src> <dst>

SUSPENDED
    This command currently refuses to do anything. A published build puts
    its own binaries in '/lib/EmuSen', inside this root - see 'man hier' -
    and nothing in this shell tells the install apart from your data, so
    'mv' could quietly relocate the running application out from under
    itself. Parked until the tree distinguishes the two. Everything below
    describes what it does when re-enabled.

DESCRIPTION
    Moves/renames a real file or directory. If <dst> is an existing
    directory, <src> lands inside it ('mv foo.txt logs/' -> 'logs/foo.txt'),
    matching real mv. An existing destination FILE is silently overwritten
    (no -i prompt anywhere in this shell, by design). Both <src> and <dst>
    must resolve inside the project's own directory tree - see 'cd'.

EXAMPLES
    mv scratch.txt home/Logs/
status 0
action none
end

case the page for cp
target none
covers 1
$ man cp
out 607
NAME
    cp - copy a file or directory

SYNOPSIS
    cp <src> <dst>
    cp -r <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    Copies a real file. If <dst> is an existing directory, <src> lands
    inside it ('cp foo.txt logs/' -> 'logs/foo.txt'), matching real cp; an
    existing destination FILE is silently overwritten (no -i prompt anywhere
    in this shell, same as 'mv'). A directory requires -r, which copies it
    and everything inside it. Both <src> and <dst> must resolve inside the
    project's own directory tree - see 'cd'.

EXAMPLES
    cp notes.txt notes.bak.txt
    cp -r home/Logs/Run1 home/Logs/Run1Backup
status 0
action none
end

case the page for rm
target none
covers 1
$ man rm
out 1288
NAME
    rm - delete a file or directory

SYNOPSIS
    rm <path>
    rm -r <path>

SUSPENDED
    This command currently refuses to do anything. A published build puts
    its own binaries in '/lib/EmuSen', inside this root - see 'man hier' -
    and nothing in this shell tells the install apart from your data, so
    'rm -r /lib' would delete the running application. Parked until the
    tree distinguishes the two. Everything below describes what it does
    when re-enabled.

DESCRIPTION
    Deletes a real file. A directory requires -r (matching real rm's own
    refusal to remove a directory without it); with -r, the directory and
    everything inside it is deleted, no further confirmation (no -i prompt
    anywhere in this shell, by design, same as 'mv'). Must resolve inside
    the project's own directory tree - see 'cd' - and refuses to remove that
    root directory itself even with -r, so 'rm -r .' from the project root
    (or 'rm -r' on the root's own path) can't wipe out the whole project.
    Like 'cd', rm only ever takes one path, so a directory name with spaces
    doesn't need quoting ('rm -r My Folder' works) - everything after the
    optional -r is treated as the literal path.

EXAMPLES
    rm scratch.txt
    rm -r home/Logs/OldRun
    rm -r My Folder
status 0
action none
end

case the page for true
