case the page for test
target none
covers 1
$ man test
out 1049
NAME
    test, [ - evaluate a condition

SYNOPSIS
    test EXPR
    [ EXPR ]

DESCRIPTION
    The main way an if/while condition does anything besides check another
    command's own exit code. A deliberately small subset of real test(1):
    a single VALUE (true if non-empty); -z/-n VALUE (string emptiness); -f/-d
    PATH (file/directory existence, with PATH read as every path in this
    shell is - '/' is this shell's root, and a path above the root is
    false, never a question about the machine's own files; before
    2026-10-10 PATH was the machine's); VALUE = / != VALUE (string comparison);
    VALUE -eq/-ne/-lt/-le/-gt/-ge VALUE (numeric comparison); a leading '!'
    negates any of the above. No -a/-o/parenthesized compound expressions -
    combine conditions with the shell's own && / || instead ('[ -f a ] && [
    -f b ]'). '[' is the same evaluator under a different name - the
    trailing ']' is required, matching real bash.

EXAMPLES
    if [ -f save.state ]; then echo found; fi
    if test $X -eq 5; then echo five; fi
status 0
action none
end

