case the page for history
target none
covers 1
$ man history
out 666
NAME
    history - list previously executed commands

SYNOPSIS
    history
    history clear
    !N
    !!

DESCRIPTION
    Lists every completed (non-buffered) command line typed at the prompt,
    numbered from 1 like bash's own history builtin. 'history clear' forgets
    all of it. '!N' re-runs history entry N; '!!' re-runs the last command -
    both are expanded before lexing even happens, the same point a real
    shell expands '!'-references, and only at the start of a brand-new
    (not mid-block) line. A script run via 'source' does NOT add its own
    lines to history - only the 'source' invocation itself does.

EXAMPLES
    history
    !!
    !12
status 0
action none
end

case the page for state
target none
covers 1
$ man state
out 1008
NAME
    state - save or load emulator state

SYNOPSIS
    state save [path]
    state load [path]

DESCRIPTION
    Saves or loads a full snapshot of emulator state (not battery-backed
    cartridge SRAM, which persists separately) - the same operation each
    frontend's own Save State/Load State hotkey or menu item performs.
    'path' defaults to this frontend's own save-state path (one slot per
    ROM) if omitted.

    A path that is given is read as every path in this shell is: relative
    to the current directory, or with a leading '/' from this shell's
    root, and refused if it lands above the root (see 'man hier'); the
    frontend is handed the resolved path. The default is the frontend's
    own and is used as the frontend gives it, since Preferences may put
    save states outside the root. Before 2026-10-10 a given path was
    passed on unchecked, so a state could be written anywhere.

EXAMPLES
    state save
    state load
    state save "/Saves/Save States/before-boss.state"
status 0
action none
end

case the page for pause
target none
covers 1
$ man pause
out 1881
NAME
    pause - stop the emulation thread so its state can be read safely

SYNOPSIS
    pause

DESCRIPTION
    EmuSen.Mistress only, and it exists because of that frontend's
    threading model rather than because pausing is a feature. Its console
    window runs on the UI thread while RunFrame runs concurrently on a
    separate emulation thread, with no synchronization between them - so a
    command that reads or writes CPU/bus/renderer state races the core
    unless that thread is actually stopped. 'pause' stops it; 'resume'
    starts it again. Already-paused and not-paused are both reported and
    are not errors.

    EmuSen.Hotaru registers neither command and needs neither: its debug
    prompt runs ON the emulation thread, so the prompt and RunFrame can
    never overlap by construction.

    Why a command, rather than pausing automatically whenever the console
    window is open: opening a window does not make anything safe, only
    actually pausing does, and leaving the game running while the console
    merely sits open is a reasonable thing to want - typing up a command
    while watching gameplay, then pausing immediately before running the
    one that touches live state.

    Both commands reach the window through plain constructor-injected
    delegates rather than a reference to it, so neither knows anything
    about that type beyond 'something I can pause and resume'. This is
    also why they cannot live in the core-agnostic command registry: they
    do not act on an IDebugTarget at all, they act on a frontend's own
    emulation thread, which IDebugTarget deliberately has no concept of.

    'Apply Cheats' in the Active Cheats window relies on this: while
    paused the emulation thread is parked outside the core, so the poke
    happens immediately instead of being queued for the next frame.

EXAMPLES
    pause
    resume
status 0
action none
end

case the page for resume
target none
covers 1
$ man resume
out 571
NAME
    resume - resume emulation after an interactive halt

SYNOPSIS
    resume
    continue
    c

DESCRIPTION
    Resumes emulation after a halt (an interactive debug prompt, a
    breakpoint, a single-step) - 'continue' and 'c' are recognized as plain
    aliases, not separate commands. EmuSen.Mistress registers its own
    pause/resume-aware 'resume' instead (its console window runs on a
    separate thread from emulation, unlike EmuSen.Hotaru's console, which
    shares the emulation thread and needs no pause/resume signal at all).

EXAMPLES
    resume
    c
status 0
action none
end

