case the page for core
target none
covers 1
$ man core
out 3215
NAME
    core - load or swap the running ROM, or start a console with nothing in it

SYNOPSIS
    core <corename> <path>
    core <corename> --console

DESCRIPTION
    Validates <corename> against the known core registry and <path> against
    that core's supported file extensions, then loads it - reloading
    VenusCore in place if a ROM is already running (a fresh SnesDebugTarget/
    DianaShInterpreter/FrameRecorder get rebuilt afterward; watches/
    breakpoints/cheats registered against the OLD ROM don't survive a swap,
    matching EmuSen.Mistress's own already-accepted 'shell-level state
    resets on reload' convention). Refuses to swap while a frame recording
    is in progress - stop it first (F6). Only registered where a session
    actually exists to reload - EmuSen.Hotaru's own pre-window launch shell
    has a separate, simpler 'core' handler of its own for resolving the
    FIRST ROM (see EmuSen_Frontend_Driver.md §3), since there's nothing to
    reload yet at that point.

WHERE THE ROM MAY BE
    <path> is read as every path in this shell is: relative to the
    current directory, or with a leading '/' from this shell's root, and
    refused if it lands above the root (see 'man hier'). There is one
    exception, because a ROM library usually lives outside the root: an
    absolute path inside the library configured as RomDirectory in
    Preferences (appsettings.json) is taken as it is. The library is only
    ever read, and only by 'core'; no other command reaches it. The host
    is handed the resolved path, so it does not depend on the shell's
    current directory. Hotaru's launch prompt makes the same checks, and
    a ROM named on its command line is the launcher's choice, not a path
    typed here, so it is not checked. Before 2026-10-10 <path> was
    checked only for existence, so any file of the machine could be
    named.

THE CONSOLE WITH NOTHING IN IT
    'core <corename> --console' starts the console <corename> names with
    no game, every drive empty, where an engine of this build says in its
    core info that the console starts empty (core ABI 1.11's
    starts_empty): the PlayStation, whose kernel then shows its screen
    with no disc and its memory card manager, and the PlayStation 2,
    whose own BIOS, the player's, shows its menu. A console whose BIOS is
    absent with no open replacement, as the PlayStation 2's is until its
    open BIOS is written, is refused in the core's own words rather than
    started black, and so is a game of it (Sapphire_Native.md §49.5). A
    console no engine starts empty is refused with that said. At a launch prompt the console's
    window opens; in a running session the core starts the console in
    place when it is the engine that runs it, and otherwise says to start
    it from the launch prompt. A disc goes in afterwards through the
    frontend's Insert Disc. See Diamond_Shell.md section 1.5 and
    Sapphire_Plan.md section 8 (G10).

EXAMPLES
    core playstation --console
    core sapphire --console
    core sapphire "/Games/Basalt Run.iso"
    core venus game2.smc
    core snes /Games/other-game.sfc
    core snes /home/player/Roms/SNES/other-game.sfc   (inside RomDirectory)
status 0
action none
end

case the page for tmux
target none
covers 1
$ man tmux
out 2209
NAME
    tmux - multiple independent shell sessions

SYNOPSIS
    tmux new [name]
    tmux list
    tmux switch <name>
    tmux kill <name>

DESCRIPTION
    Each session is a fully independent DianaShInterpreter - its own
    variables, command history, and $?/pending-multiline-input state -
    that you can create and switch between. 'new' with no name auto-numbers
    it (session-1, session-2, ...). All sessions observe the same one live
    core/ROM, the same way several real tmux windows attached to one real
    machine each get their own independent shell but see the same one
    machine - this is NOT multiple games running at once, just multiple
    independent shells watching the one that is.

    Because a DianaShInterpreter can't be repointed at a new target once
    built (see 'core'), swapping the ROM rebuilds every session's
    interpreter from scratch, the same 'shell-level state resets on reload'
    convention 'core' already documents - it's not just the current session
    that loses its variables/history on a ROM swap, all of them do.

    Two things deliberately SURVIVE that rebuild: a session's name, and the
    account it is running as (see 'su'). The account is carried forward
    explicitly rather than left to reset, because a ROM swap dropping every
    session back to root would be a silent privilege escalation the moment a
    permission system actually enforces anything. For the same reason a new
    session inherits whoever created it rather than starting as root, so
    'tmux new' can never be a way back to an account you left.

    Not available everywhere: a host needs to opt in by handing
    DianaShInterpreter.CreateDefault a DianaShSessionManager. Where it IS
    available, whether switching sessions is itself safe against a
    concurrently-running emulation thread is that host's own concern, not
    this command's - EmuSen.Hotaru/EmuSen.Mistress each keep a separate
    DianaShInterpreterScheduler per session for exactly that reason; the
    standalone DianaSh shell (no core, no concurrent thread) needs none at
    all.

EXAMPLES
    tmux new
    tmux new investigation
    tmux list
    tmux switch investigation
    tmux kill session-1
status 0
action none
end

case the page for ps
target none
covers 1
$ man ps
out 737
NAME
    ps, jobs - list active breakpoints, watches, and sessions

SYNOPSIS
    ps
    jobs

DESCRIPTION
    Lists every active breakpoint (see 'bp'), watch (see 'watch'), and tmux
    session (see 'tmux', if a session manager is registered for this host)
    as one unified table - the read-only counterpart to 'kill'. 'jobs' is a
    plain alias, not a separate command. Each row's id column is exactly
    what 'kill' expects: 'bp<N>' for a breakpoint, 'watch<N>' for a watch, or
    a session's own name - copy a row's id straight into 'kill' unchanged.
    Prints nothing wrong, just an empty-looking table, with no ROM loaded and
    no session manager registered (there's nothing to list, not an error).

EXAMPLES
    ps
    jobs
status 0
action none
end

case the page for kill
target none
covers 1
$ man kill
out 1060
NAME
    kill - remove a breakpoint, watch, or session by its 'ps' id

SYNOPSIS
    kill <id>

DESCRIPTION
    One verb for tearing down whatever 'ps' just showed, instead of needing
    to know which of 'bp remove'/'watch remove'/'tmux kill' owns a given id -
    all three of those still work exactly as before, this just routes to
    whichever one already owns <id>'s kind of thing, based on its shape:
    'bp<N>' removes breakpoint N, 'watch<N>' removes watch N, and anything
    else is looked up as a session name (same rules as 'tmux kill' - can't
    kill the only remaining session; killing the CURRENT session switches you
    to whichever one is left, the same hand-off 'tmux kill' already does).
    A session literally NAMED like 'bp3' or 'watch1' is only reachable this
    way by accident of that ambiguity - 'kill' always tries the breakpoint/
    watch reading of an id shaped like one first; use 'tmux kill <name>'
    directly for a session whose name collides with that shape.

EXAMPLES
    kill bp1
    kill watch3
    kill investigation
status 0
action none
end

