case the page for step
target none
covers 1
$ man step
out 2320
NAME
    step - single-step, step over a call, or step out of a routine

SYNOPSIS
    step [<count>]
    s [<count>]
    step over
    step out
    step <cpu> [<count>|over|out]

DESCRIPTION
    Arms a halt and lets emulation run just far enough to reach it, then
    halts again at the same interactive prompt - 's' is recognized as a
    plain alias, not a separate command. Needs a ROM loaded.

    Bare 'step' executes exactly one instruction. 'step <count>' executes
    <count> of them (hex, like every other address/count in this shell), for
    walking past a delay loop without holding down 's'. A real breakpoint
    that fires part-way through still wins and halts early - a multi-step is
    a convenience, not a way to suppress breakpoints.

    'step over' runs the call at the current instruction to completion and
    halts on the instruction after it. It works by depth, not by address:
    it records the call stack's current depth and halts at the first
    instruction executed once the stack is back down to it. That makes it
    correct for recursion (an inner call at the same address doesn't stop
    it early) and it degrades gracefully on a non-call instruction, where
    the depth never rises and it behaves exactly like a plain 'step'.

    'step out' is the same mechanism with a target of depth - 1: run until
    the routine currently executing returns to its caller. It errors rather
    than running away when the call stack is already empty.

    Both depend on the call stack, so both need a core that reports one -
    see 'man bt', which also covers what makes a depth reading go wrong
    (a game that manipulates its own stack) and how to resync it.

EXAMPLES
    step
    s 20
    step over
    step out
    step sa1 out

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word steps another chip - see 'man cpus'. 'over' and 'out'
    additionally need that chip to report a call stack, so on an SNES they
    work for the main CPU and the SA-1 but not the GSU or SPC700, which
    still accept a plain instruction step.

    Stepping one chip lets the others keep running - they share a timebase,
    and freezing everything but one processor would change the behaviour
    being investigated, which on a coprocessor handshake is usually the
    entire bug.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, bt, runto, bp, resume
status 0
action none
end

case the page for eval
target none
covers 1
$ man eval
out 5659
NAME
    eval - evaluate an expression against live core state

SYNOPSIS
    eval <expr>
    eval symbols [<filter>]
    eval <cpu> <expr>

DESCRIPTION
    Evaluates one integer expression against the machine as it is right
    now and prints the result three ways at once - decimal, hex, binary -
    so a flag word or a packed register never needs converting by hand.

    The same language backs conditional breakpoints ('bp add <addr> if
    <expr>'), which is the point of having it: 'eval' is where an expression
    gets checked before being trusted to gate a halt.

SYMBOLS
    'eval symbols' lists everything nameable, which is the authoritative
    answer for whatever core is loaded - the list below is the SNES core's.

    Registers   a x y s (sp) d (dp) pc pb (pbr) db (dbr) p e
    Flags       flag.c flag.z flag.i flag.d flag.x flag.m flag.v flag.n
                (each 0 or 1)
    Position    frame, scanline, cycle
    Context     opaddr (the 24-bit address of the instruction a halt is
                sitting in front of), stackdepth
    Labels      every name defined via 'label add' - see 'man label'

    A core that publishes none of its own still gets symbols for free: the
    generic fallback names every register the core already reports through
    'regs', plus 'frame' and any labels. Nothing has to be written per-core
    for 'eval' to work on a new console.

    A core of the stable ABI adds two sources to that fallback. Its console's
    system pack names symbols derived from the main processor's registers -
    on the SNES the eight flags and the four aliases above, read from P, S,
    D, PB and DB - and 'stackdepth' is the depth of the call stack the host
    keeps where the core reports calls. That stack is kept only on frames
    run with something armed, so it is empty after frames that ran plain.
    'scanline', 'cycle' and 'opaddr' are not among them: the ABI carries no
    timing view and no last-instruction address.

UNITS
    A core of the stable ABI may also list units: the parts of the machine
    that run no code, such as a PPU, an APU and its channels, or a
    cartridge's board. Their registers are symbols too, after everything
    above, in every scope ('eval', 'eval <cpu>', conditions, logpoints):

    <unit>.<register>  always, the unit's name lowercased with anything but
                       letters, digits and '_' dropped: 'ppu.PPUCTRL',
                       'pulse1.Duty', 'board.Mapper'
    <register>         alone, where only one unit has a register of that
                       name: 'PPUCTRL'

    Case does not matter. A name the processor, the system pack or a label
    already answers stays theirs, so nothing that resolved before a core
    listed units changes meaning: on the NES 'x' is the CPU's X and
    'ppu.x' the PPU's fine X, and with a label PPUCTRL defined 'PPUCTRL'
    is the label's address while 'ppu.PPUCTRL' is the register. A register
    name two units share is not a symbol alone ('Duty' is refused on the
    NES; write 'pulse1.Duty'). A name the language cannot spell, such as
    the APU's '$4000', is not a symbol; 'regs' shows it.

    A unit register is read from the core when the expression is evaluated,
    at the hit for a condition, and reading it changes nothing in the
    machine. 'eval symbols' lists the '<unit>.<register>' forms.

NUMBERS AND MEMORY
    Numbers     123 decimal, $1F or 0x1F hex, %1010 binary
    [addr]      one byte at <addr>
    {addr}      one little-endian word at <addr>
    [SPACE:addr]  read from a named space instead, e.g. [VRAM:$2760]

    Without a space name, a read goes to the CPU bus. On the SNES core an
    address that lands in WRAM is served straight from the RAM array
    instead, which is both faster and free of side effects - so the common
    case ('[$7E0020]') is always safe to put in a breakpoint condition.
    An address that does NOT resolve to WRAM goes through the live bus, and
    reading a hardware register there has whatever side effect the real
    register has (RDNMI clears the pending-NMI flag, OPHCT/OPVCT toggle a
    byte-order latch). Name a plain space explicitly when that matters.

OPERATORS
    Lowest to highest precedence:
        ||  &&  |  ^  &  == !=  < > <= >=  << >>  + -  * / %
    Unary: - ! ~ . Parentheses group. '&&' and '||' short-circuit, so
    '[$7E0000] != 0 && 100 / [$7E0000] > 2' is safe. Any nonzero value is
    true; comparisons produce 1 or 0.

QUOTING
    '&&', '||', '<', '>' and '|' are the shell's own operators and are
    consumed before this command sees them - quote any expression using
    them. Everything after 'eval' is rejoined with single spaces, so simple
    unquoted forms ('eval a + 1') work.

EXAMPLES
    eval a
    eval [$7E0020]
    eval {$7E13C6} + 4
    eval "flag.m == 0 && a > $100"
    eval [VRAM:$2760]
    eval symbols flag
    eval gsu r14
    eval spc "a == $F0"

OTHER PROCESSORS
    'eval <cpu> <expr>' evaluates against another chip's registers - see
    'man cpus'. Its symbols are that chip's own, so 'eval gsu r14' reads
    the GSU's R14 while 'eval a' still reads the main CPU's accumulator,
    and an unnamed memory read like '[$8000]' resolves in that chip's own
    code space rather than the main CPU bus.

    A chip name alone is an EXPRESSION, not a scope: 'eval a' evaluates the
    symbol 'a'. The scope reading only applies when something follows it.

    Symbols for a chip come from whatever registers it already reports, so
    'eval <cpu> symbols' is the reliable way to see what is nameable rather
    than guessing at a register's spelling.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, bp, regs, mem, label
status 0
action none
end

case the page for bt
target none
covers 1
$ man bt
out 2393
NAME
    bt - backtrace the live call chain

SYNOPSIS
    bt [<count>]
    bt reset
    bt <cpu> [<count>|reset]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the calls currently on the stack, innermost frame first: what was
    called, where it was called from, and which frame number the call
    happened on. This is the dynamic counterpart to 'callers' (see 'man
    callers'), and the two answer genuinely different questions. 'callers'
    scans code and reports every instruction that COULD reach an address;
    'bt' reports the one path that actually did, this time. When a routine
    has six static callers, 'callers' gives you six suspects and 'bt' gives
    you the answer.

    Frames are recorded from the CPU's own call and return opcodes plus its
    interrupt entry points, so an NMI or IRQ frame is labelled as such
    rather than looking like an ordinary call. Addresses are resolved
    through the label registry - see 'man label'.

ACCURACY
    A call stack tracked this way is an inference, not machine state, and
    it can drift on code that manipulates its own stack directly: a routine
    that pushes a return address and jumps, that pulls a return address it
    never intends to return through, or that unwinds several frames with a
    stack-pointer write rather than matched returns. Two symptoms give this
    away - a depth that only ever grows, and a rising 'unmatched returns'
    count, which 'bt' reports whenever it is nonzero.

    'bt reset' forgets the current chain and starts counting again from the
    current instruction. Do that after stepping through a routine known to
    play with its own stack, and before trusting 'step out' or a 'profile'
    run that has to survive it. Depth is also capped, so a runaway chain
    stops recording rather than growing without bound.

    Frames opened before the debug target attached are not on the stack -
    code already running at that moment shows up in 'profile' under
    '(outside any recorded call)' rather than under its real caller.

EXAMPLES
    bt
    bt 8
    bt reset

OTHER PROCESSORS
    'bt <cpu>' backtraces another chip - see 'man cpus'. Only a chip with a
    real call/return seam has a stack to report: on an SNES that is the
    main CPU and the SA-1, both 65816s. The GSU and the SPC700 report none,
    and say so rather than inventing frames.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, callers, step, profile, bp, label
status 0
action none
end

case the page for cpus
target none
covers 1
$ man cpus
out 4529
NAME
    cpus - list the processors a debug command can be aimed at

SYNOPSIS
    cpus

DESCRIPTION
    A console is rarely one processor. An SNES is always at least two - the
    65816 main CPU and the SPC700 driving sound - and a cartridge can add a
    third: an SA-1, a SuperFX GSU, a NEC DSP. Each runs its own code, in its
    own address space, from its own program counter. An SA-1 game's $00:82D7
    is simply not the same instruction as the main CPU's $00:82D7.

    'cpus' lists the ones this core publishes, with what each supports, and
    every scoped command takes one of those names as an optional first word:

        bp sa1 add 82D7            breakpoint on the coprocessor's PC
        cov gsu on                 coverage of the GSU's instruction stream
        bt sa1                     the coprocessor's own call chain
        step spc over              step the sound CPU over a call
        eval gsu r14 > 100         evaluate against the GSU's registers
        regs spc                   just that chip's registers
        disasm gsu                 disassemble from where the GSU is now

    With no scope word every command means the main CPU, so nothing that
    worked before needs rewriting. 'cop' is still accepted as an alias for
    whichever cartridge coprocessor is present, from before chips had names:
    the first listed after the main processor that is not the SPC700.

THE MAIN PROCESSOR
    The main processor is the first one 'cpus' lists. The order is the
    core's statement, not the shell's guess: IDebugTarget.DebugCpus is
    documented as main CPU first, and a v1 core's machine info lists its
    processors main processor first (EmuSen_CoreAPI.md §6.4). The word
    'cpu' always names that processor, whatever the core calls it, so
    'regs cpu' and 'disasm cpu' mean the same on every core; a core's own
    name for it ('m68k' on the Genesis) works as well. Whether a scoped
    command speaks of a coprocessor is decided by position too, not by the
    name. Until 2026-10-10 both were decided by the name 'cpu', so on
    Nephrite, whose 68000 is 'm68k', 'regs cpu' and 'disasm cpu' refused,
    a 68000 breakpoint was reported as a coprocessor's, and 'cop' meant
    the 68000.

WHAT EACH CHIP SUPPORTS
    Support is not uniform, because the hardware is not uniform, and 'cpus'
    prints per chip what is actually wired rather than letting a command
    fail obscurely later:

        bp      the core can halt this chip mid-instruction
        cov     its executed addresses can be recorded
        bt      it has a call/return seam to infer a stack from
        regs    it reports named registers
        disasm  it has a code space and a disassembler for its ISA

    One absence is worth knowing about. The GSU and the SPC700 have no
    call stack: the GSU's LINK/subroutine convention is register-based
    rather than a stack the way the 65816's JSR/RTS is, so there is no
    honest seam to infer frames from, and 'bt gsu' says so instead of
    inventing them.

    The SA-1 gets everything, because it IS a 65816: the same call/return
    and interrupt seams the main CPU uses apply to it unchanged, so
    'bt sa1', 'step sa1 out' and 'profile sa1' all work.

    The NEC DSP gets everything too, by a different route. Its firmware
    runs from a mask ROM the cartridge never exposes, so there is no code
    to read - but the chip is still stepped one instruction at a time by
    this core, and that is all a breakpoint needs. Its addresses are word
    indices, not bytes, because that is what the DSP's own PC is: 'bp dsp
    add 100' means the 257th instruction, and 'disasm DSPPRG' indexes the
    same way. Its CALL/RET pair drives a real call stack, so 'bt dsp' and
    'cov dsp funcs' map firmware nobody has source for.

HALTING, AND WHICH CHIP RESUMES
    Only one chip halts at a time. Whichever one hit its breakpoint is the
    one that skips a check on resume, so 'continue' leaves the breakpoint it
    is sitting on instead of instantly re-halting - and, just as important,
    the OTHER chips do not skip theirs. Arming the main CPU's resume flag
    for a coprocessor halt would let the coprocessor re-break immediately
    on the same PC, forever.

    A halt mid-frame keeps the unspent clock budget of whichever chip was
    running, so resuming continues the frame rather than restarting it.

EXAMPLES
    cpus
    bp gsu add 008010
    cov sa1 on
    regs dsp

SEE ALSO
    bp, cov, bt, step, profile, eval, regs, disasm, copflow
status 0
action none
end

case the page for copflow
target none
covers 1
$ man copflow
out 2416
NAME
    copflow - watch the CPU and a coprocessor talk

SYNOPSIS
    copflow on [<size>]
    copflow off
    copflow clear
    copflow tail [<n>]
    copflow stats
    copflow poll

DESCRIPTION
    Every cartridge coprocessor talks to the main CPU through one narrow
    register window, and that window is where coprocessor bugs actually
    live. The chip itself is usually fine. What breaks is the conversation:
    the CPU writes a parameter block and kicks the chip, the chip works and
    sets a status bit, the CPU polls that bit and moves on. Any one of those
    four steps can fail silently, and none of them are visible in a register
    dump - by the time you look, the moment has passed.

    'copflow' logs that window: every read and write, with the value, the
    frame it happened on, and which side did it. 'copflow tail' replays the
    recent conversation in order, which is usually enough to see which of
    the four steps never happened.

POLL RUNS
    'copflow poll' exists for the most common coprocessor failure by a wide
    margin: the CPU asks a question the chip never answers. It tracks the
    longest run of consecutive reads of one register where the value never
    changed, and reports the run in progress right now.

    A long run is unambiguous. If the game read $3030 ninety thousand times
    in a row and always got the same byte back, the game is not slow and the
    plot is not wrong - it is spinning on a status bit that this core never
    updates. That points at the register model, not the chip's arithmetic,
    and it is a very different bug from one 'regs' or 'cophist' would find.

    A write by either side always breaks the run, because a write is
    progress: it means someone learned something and acted. Only unbroken
    reads of an unchanging value count.

COST
    Disarmed, this costs one bool test per coprocessor register access -
    nothing measurable, and nothing at all on a cartridge with no
    coprocessor, which never reaches the seam. Armed, it keeps whole-run
    tallies per register plus a ring of the most recent accesses, so a long
    session cannot grow memory without bound; 'copflow tail' can only show
    what is still in the ring, while 'copflow stats' and 'copflow poll'
    cover the entire armed run.

EXAMPLES
    copflow on
    copflow tail 40
    copflow poll
    copflow stats

SEE ALSO
    cpus, cophist, regs, watch, bp
status 0
action none
end

case the page for dma
target none
covers 1
$ man dma
out 3144
NAME
    dma - what the block-transfer engine is set up to do, and what it did

SYNOPSIS
    dma
    dma log on [<n>]
    dma log off
    dma log [<n>]
    dma log clear
    dma stats

DESCRIPTION
    On a SNES nearly everything the player sees arrives by DMA. Tiles,
    palettes, sprite tables and the sound driver's samples are all block
    transfers, and per-scanline effects - a status-bar split, a colour
    gradient, a growing window wipe - are HDMA. So a large class of visual
    bug is not a rendering bug at all: the data never arrived, or arrived
    somewhere else, and the renderer faithfully drew what it was given.

    'dma' alone prints the channel table: for each of the eight channels,
    its direction, its B-bus write pattern, which register it targets (by
    name, not just an address), where it reads from, how long the transfer
    is, and for HDMA the table pointer and line counter it has reached. An
    idle channel still shows its last configuration, which is usually the
    thing you wanted to see.

    On a SNES engine of the stable ABI the table is the system pack's
    reading of the registers as last written, $4300-$437F with MDMAEN and
    HDMAEN, from the engine's IO space. An enabled HDMA channel is shown
    'on' while its line counter is not zero and 'arm' once it is, since a
    header byte of 0 ends a channel until the next frame (fullsnes); a
    counter the program itself wrote to 0 reads as ended too. The indirect
    address is $43x5-$43x7, the byte count's own register, as on the
    hardware. 'dma log' needs a transfer record the stable ABI does not
    carry, and refuses there.

TRANSFER LOG
    'dma log on' records every transfer as it happens - channel, kind,
    destination, source, length, and the frame and scanline it ran on.
    This is the part the channel table cannot give you, because a general
    DMA reconfigures and fires many times a frame and the table only ever
    shows the last one.

    The scanline column is what makes it worth reading. A VRAM transfer
    during vblank is normal; the same transfer at scanline 100 is a bug
    that will show as corrupt graphics, and 'dma log' names the frame it
    first happened on. Pair it with 'bp when ppuaccess' to halt on one.

BANDWIDTH
    'dma stats' totals the whole armed run two ways: per channel, split
    into general and HDMA because those cost very differently, and per
    destination register, which answers 'where is the bandwidth going'.
    A game that is slow during one scene and not another is usually
    uploading something enormous, and the destination table names it.

COST
    Disarmed the log is a null field test at the end of each transfer, so
    a normal run pays nothing. Armed, it is a fixed-size ring plus a set
    of counters, so a long session cannot grow memory without bound -
    'dma log' shows only what is still in the ring, while 'dma stats'
    covers the whole armed run. The channel table is read on demand and
    costs nothing at all when not being run.

EXAMPLES
    dma
    dma log on
    dma log 40
    dma stats

SEE ALSO
    bp, copflow, mem, watch, tilemap
status 0
action none
end

case the page for label
target none
covers 1
$ man label
out 2309
NAME
    label - name addresses, so an investigation's findings survive it

SYNOPSIS
    label add <addr> <name> [<comment...>]
    label list [<filter>]
    label at <addr>
    label remove <name|addr>
    label clear
    label load <path>
    label save <path>

DESCRIPTION
    Attaches a name (and optionally a comment) to an address. Once named,
    that address reads as '$00A3B2 <NmiHandler>' everywhere it appears -
    'disasm', 'bt', 'bp list', 'counters top', 'profile top' - and the name
    becomes usable as a symbol in any expression, so 'bp add NmiHandler'
    and 'eval [PlayerX]' work.

    This is the piece that makes a long investigation compound instead of
    restarting. Without it, every session re-derives what $7E13C6 was, from
    notes kept somewhere outside the tool. 'label save' writes the set out
    and 'label load' merges one back in, so the naming survives a restart
    and can be committed alongside whatever else an investigation produced.

    'disasm' prints a label on its own line above the instruction it names,
    the way a disassembly listing does, and annotates any operand whose
    statically-resolvable target is itself labelled.

    'label at <addr>' answers the reverse question - given an address in the
    middle of a routine, which routine is it in? An exact hit prints the
    name; otherwise it reports the nearest label at or below the address
    with the offset ('MainLoop+12').

    The map is one-to-one in both directions: renaming an address drops its
    old name, and pointing an existing name at a new address moves it
    rather than leaving two entries.

FILE FORMAT
    One entry per line, '<hex address> <name> [comment...]'. Blank lines and
    lines starting with '#' are ignored. '$' and '0x' prefixes on the
    address are accepted. Deliberately its own trivial format rather than
    any one assembler's symbol file, since nothing here knows which
    assembler a given ROM was built with; a real .sym/.mlb file converts
    with one 'awk' line. Paths resolve inside this shell's sandbox - see
    'man hier'.

EXAMPLES
    label add 00A3B2 NmiHandler main vblank entry
    label add 7E13C6 CoinCount
    label at 00A3C0
    label list Nmi
    label save /Usr/Home/Documents/smw.labels

SEE ALSO
    disasm, bt, bp, eval, callers
status 0
action none
end

case the page for runto
target none
covers 1
$ man runto
out 2218
NAME
    runto - resume until a named event instead of an address

SYNOPSIS
    runto nmi | irq | brk | cop
    runto scanline <n>
    runto frame [<n>]
    runto <addr>

DESCRIPTION
    Resumes emulation and halts at the next occurrence of an event, rather
    than requiring an address to put a breakpoint on. Half of debugging a
    console is 'get me to the interesting moment', and for a whole class of
    moments there is no single address to break on - the vblank handler's
    entry address is not knowable before you've found it, and 'the state at
    scanline 100' is not an address at all.

    'runto nmi|irq|brk|cop' halts as soon as the CPU takes an interrupt of
    that kind, with the halt landing inside the handler. Pair it with 'bt',
    which then shows what the interrupt interrupted, and 'label add opaddr
    NmiHandler' to keep the address you just found.

    'runto scanline <n>' halts at the start of scanline <n> of whichever
    frame is in progress, which is how you catch a mid-frame register write
    (a status-bar split, a mid-screen mode change) in the act. Scanline
    numbers here are decimal.

    'runto frame [<n>]' halts once frame <n> has completed; with no argument
    it means the next frame, which is the fastest way to advance exactly one
    frame from a halt.

    Events are noticed where they happen but the halt lands at the next
    instruction boundary, because that is the only place a core can safely
    stop - the same one-instruction lag 'bp write' has, for the same reason.

    'runto <addr>' is a convenience for a plain address: it adds an ordinary
    breakpoint and resumes. That breakpoint is NOT removed when it fires -
    the command reports its id so 'bp remove <id>' can clear it. A one-shot
    breakpoint would need a concept the registry doesn't have, and silently
    leaving a permanent breakpoint behind would be worse than saying so.

    An armed run-to survives a plain 'continue': it fires whenever the event
    next happens. Only one run-to of each kind can be armed at a time -
    arming a second replaces the first.

EXAMPLES
    runto nmi
    runto scanline 100
    runto frame
    runto 808000

SEE ALSO
    step, bp, bt, resume
status 0
action none
end

case the page for counters
target none
covers 1
$ man counters
out 2382
NAME
    counters - per-address read/write/execute tallies

SYNOPSIS
    counters on <space>
    counters off
    counters clear
    counters <addr> [<len>]
    counters top [r|w|x|u] [<n>]
    counters cold <addr> <len>

DESCRIPTION
    Counts every read, write and execute per address in one memory space.
    Where 'watch' records individual events with context and 'cov' records
    only whether an address ever executed, this records how MANY times -
    which is the question behind 'is this table live or dead', 'which byte
    of this struct does the game actually touch', and 'which half of this
    buffer is the one being updated'.

    Armed against one space at a time, because the tallies cost four arrays
    the size of that space - worth paying where a question is being asked,
    not worth paying everywhere by default. 'counters off' stops counting
    but leaves what was collected readable; 'counters clear' zeroes the
    counts and stays armed.

    A bare address (with an optional length) reports totals over that range,
    including how many of its bytes were touched at all. 'counters top'
    ranks the busiest addresses - 'r' reads (default), 'w' writes, 'x'
    executes, 'u' uninitialized reads. 'counters cold' is the inverse:
    addresses in a range that nothing ever touched, which is what proves a
    table is unused rather than merely quiet.

UNINITIALIZED READS
    An address read before anything wrote it (since counting started) is
    counted separately. On real hardware that read returns whatever the RAM
    powered up holding, so a game doing it is either relying on
    power-on state or has a genuine bug - and an emulator whose RAM fill
    differs from hardware will diverge exactly there. 'counters top u' is
    the fastest way to find those addresses.

    'Since counting started' is doing real work in that sentence: arm before
    the moment being studied, not after, or an address the game initialized
    earlier will look uninitialized.

    Execution is counted against whatever space the core reports its program
    counter in (the CPU bus), so counting a RAM space leaves the execute
    column at zero unless that RAM is genuinely executed from.

EXAMPLES
    counters on WRAM
    counters 13c6 8
    counters top w 10
    counters top u
    counters cold 1000 200

SEE ALSO
    watch, cov, search, memfind, profile
status 0
action none
end

case the page for freeze
target none
covers 1
$ man freeze
out 2266
NAME
    freeze - pin an address by undoing every write to it

SYNOPSIS
    freeze add <space> <addr> [<value>]
    freeze list
    freeze remove <id>
    freeze clear

DESCRIPTION
    Holds an address at a value by writing that value straight back the
    instant anything writes something else. Without a <value> it pins
    whatever the address holds right now, which is the common case: find an
    address with 'search', freeze it, watch what stops moving.

    This is a debugging instrument, not a cheat. 'cheat poke' re-applies
    once per frame, so the game's own value is live for most of that frame
    and every read in between sees it; a freeze undoes the write
    immediately, so nothing ever observes the value the game tried to store.
    That difference is the whole point when the question is 'what breaks if
    this counter never changes' or 'is this the variable driving that
    animation' - a per-frame poke answers neither cleanly.

    The restore is itself a write, and is suppressed from re-entering the
    freeze check, so it cannot recurse. Writes matching the frozen value are
    left alone and not counted. 'freeze list' reports how many writes each
    entry has undone, which doubles as a cheap 'is anything even writing
    here' signal - a freeze with a blocked count of zero means the address
    you suspected is not the one being written.

    Only spaces the core reports writes for can be frozen; freezing an
    address in a read-only space is refused rather than silently ignored.
    Freezing an address whose writes the core does not observe will also
    show a blocked count of zero - see 'man watch' for which paths report.

ON A CORE OF THE STABLE ABI
    Such a core reports a store only after its frame, so the host cannot
    undo a write as it happens. It reads each frozen byte at the end of
    every frame and writes the value back where it has changed, as it
    applies cheats. The game can therefore read its own value within the
    frame, as with a per-frame poke, and the count in 'freeze list' is of
    frames whose end found the byte changed, not of writes.

EXAMPLES
    freeze add WRAM 13c6
    freeze add WRAM 0dbf 63
    freeze list
    freeze clear

SEE ALSO
    cheat, watch, search, counters
status 0
action none
end

case the page for profile
target none
covers 1
$ man profile
out 2095
NAME
    profile - which routines the instruction budget actually goes to

SYNOPSIS
    profile on
    profile off
    profile clear
    profile top [<n>]
    profile <cpu> on|off|clear|top [<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Charges every executed instruction to whichever routine is innermost on
    the call stack at the time, then ranks routines by the count. That makes
    the figure EXCLUSIVE: a routine that spends all its time inside a call
    it made scores low, and the callee scores high. Inclusive ('this
    routine and everything under it') is deliberately not reported, because
    on a stack that can drift (see 'man bt') an inclusive number compounds
    the drift while an exclusive one localizes it.

    The unit is instructions, not cycles. Instructions are what the
    per-instruction seam this rides on can count exactly; a cycle figure
    would have to be attributed across a boundary the seam doesn't see. For
    'which routine is hot', the two rank almost identically.

    Counting only happens while armed, so 'profile on' ... 'profile off'
    brackets the window being studied. Both the call count and the
    instruction count are reported per routine, so a routine that is hot
    because it is slow is distinguishable from one that is hot because it is
    called constantly.

    Instructions executed while nothing is on the call stack are charged to
    '(outside any recorded call)'. A large share there is normal and means
    the code was already running when profiling started - it is not a bug,
    but it is a reason to arm profiling from a known point and to run 'bt
    reset' first if the stack looks wrong.

    This profiles the GAME, not the emulator. For where EmuSen's own frame
    time goes, see 'perf' and 'coretop'.

EXAMPLES
    profile on
    profile top 10
    profile off
    profile sa1 top 10

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word profiles another chip - see 'man cpus'. Since this is
    built on the call stack, it only works for a chip that reports one: on
    an SNES, the main CPU and the SA-1.

SEE ALSO
    cpus, bt, counters, cov, coretop
status 0
action none
end

case the page for hotaru
target none
covers 1
$ man hotaru
out 2078
NAME
    hotaru - play a ROM in Hotaru's game window

SYNOPSIS
    hotaru [--output window] <rom>

DESCRIPTION
    Opens Hotaru's game window on <rom>, the core chosen by its file
    extension, with everything that window has always brought: the F-key
    hotkeys (F12 lists them), 'feed', 'coretop -w' and the other debug
    windows, rewind and fast-forward. The shell keeps its prompt: from the
    terminal, the prompt that follows is the running game's own, where
    'core' swaps the ROM; in the shell's own window, that window is handed
    to the game's interpreter. Closing the game ends the program, as
    closing Hotaru always did.

    --output says where the game's picture goes. 'window' is the default
    and, for now, the only value; a picture drawn in the terminal is
    planned as a second (EmuSen_DianaSh.md section 18), and will be added
    as a new value without changing the command.

    <rom> is read as every path in this shell is, relative to the current
    directory or with a leading '/' from this shell's root, and refused if
    it lands above the root, except that an absolute path inside the ROM
    library configured as RomDirectory is taken as it is - the rule
    'core' follows (see 'man core'). A ROM that will not load is reported
    as '[CPU HALT]' with the reason, and the prompt stays.

WHERE IT IS OFFERED
    'hotaru' is one of EmuSen's commands, not the shell's: DianaSh itself
    knows no emulator. The EmuSen shell program, emusen-shell, offers it,
    and 'emusen-shell game.sfc' is shorthand for starting the program and
    typing 'hotaru game.sfc' - with the difference that a path given on the
    program's command line is the launcher's choice and is checked only
    for existence. Inside a running game the command refuses, since that
    shell already has its game, and Mistress's console does not offer it
    at all, since Mistress runs its own.

EXAMPLES
    hotaru /Games/smw.sfc
    hotaru --output window /home/player/Roms/SNES/smw.sfc   (inside RomDirectory)
    emusen-shell /home/player/Roms/SNES/smw.sfc
status 0
action none
end

case the page for dupes
target none
covers 1
$ man dupes
out 3302
NAME
    dupes - find new code that duplicates code already in the repository

SYNOPSIS
    dupes [--base <rev>] [--min-tokens N] [--warn-tokens N]
          [--lang cs|rust|all] [--repo <dir>]

DESCRIPTION
    The development tool of the emusen.dev module, linked into the
    standalone dianash program and not into the platform library. It
    runs PMD's copy-paste detector (CPD) over the repository's tracked
    .rs and .cs files and prints each duplicated block, one line each:

        file:first-last  duplicates  file:first-last  (N tokens)

    With --base <rev> only the blocks with a place that overlaps a line
    added between <rev> and HEAD (git diff -U0 <rev>..HEAD) are
    reported, the added place first. A place is a new copy when the
    added lines cover at least half of its lines; one that a change only
    touches prints as 'warning: touches an existing duplicate: <line>'
    and never fails the run. Without it the whole tree is an
    audit, a block's first place against each of its others, ranked by
    tokens times places.

    --min-tokens (default 100) is the size at which a finding fails the
    run; --warn-tokens (default 75) is the least size CPD is asked for.
    A finding from the warning size up to the minimum prints as
    'warning: <line>' and does not fail. --lang limits the run to one
    language; C# is read with CPD's --ignore-usings.

    The summary goes to standard error. The exit status is 1 when a
    finding is at or over --min-tokens, 0 otherwise, and 2 when the
    command could not run: PMD missing, git failing, a bad option, a
    malformed dupes.accept.

    CPD is the program named by EMUSEN_CPD, by default
    ~/.cache/emusen/toolchains/pmd-7.28.0/bin/pmd. PMD is a developer's
    tool and is never shipped. The repository is --repo, else
    EMUSEN_REPO, else the one the process stands in; the standalone
    shell moves its own directory, so name it there. The module reads
    the repository directly, not through the shell's root.

    dupes.ignore, at the repository root, lists what is not compared:
    one pattern a line, # for comments. A pattern is matched against
    consecutive parts of a path, * standing for any text within a part:
    'tests' ignores every folder or file of that name, 'board_chip.rs' a
    file, 'Cores/Sega/gen' that folder. The list as measured on
    2026-10-27 is generated tables (board_chip.rs, board_fm.rs), tests,
    examples, benches, the WiseMan harness, build output and the
    reference trees.

    dupes.accept, beside it, records a duplicate the project keeps. One
    line: the two places exactly as dupes prints them, two spaces, and
    the reason, which is required:

        a/x.rs:5-14  duplicates  b/y.rs:40-49  one table, two ports

    A pair is skipped while both places stand at those lines, either way
    round; if either moves, the finding comes back to be accepted again
    or removed.

    CPD works on the files as they stand, so
    uncommitted edits shift the lines the diff names; run it on a
    committed tree.

EXAMPLES
    Check what the last merge added:

        EMUSEN_REPO=$PWD dianash -c 'dupes --base WiseMan~1'

    Audit the whole tree, Rust only, with a stricter limit:

        EMUSEN_REPO=$PWD dianash -c 'dupes --lang rust --min-tokens 150'
status 0
action none
end

case the page for chd
target none
covers 1
$ man chd
out 5111
NAME
    chd - convert the library's disc games to CHD, check them, list them

SYNOPSIS
    chd ls [<path>...]
    chd convert <path>... [--fast] [-j <threads>]
    chd check [<path>...]
    chd wait [chd<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The CHD tool of the emusen.library module. A <path> is a file or a
    folder of the library, AppSettings.RomDirectory, written relative to
    it with / between its parts: psx, "psx/Rayman (USA)", SCD. / is the
    whole library, and so is no path for ls and check. The disc games
    under each path are its PlayStation and Sega CD discs: a cue sheet
    and its tracks, a lone raw track, an ISO image, or a zip holding one.
    Which console a file is for is the library scan's own routing, then
    the nearest console folder above it (psx, segacd).

    THE LIBRARY IS ONLY READ. Nothing under it is written, renamed or
    removed: no CHD, no package, no link. A batch whose output folders and
    the library overlap is refused before anything is written.

    'convert' converts each disc with chdman, MAME's CHD manager, which a
    build ships beside the platform library. The CHD goes to Compressed,
    at the disc's path in the library with .chd (psx/Rayman (USA).cue
    becomes Compressed/psx/Rayman (USA).chd), first as a partial file
    that is renamed into place only when three checks pass in the same
    run:

        1  chdman's own verify: every hunk decompressed, the overall SHA-1
           compared;
        2  every track rebuilt from the CHD through EmuSen's own reader,
           a reader other than chdman's, and its CRC-32, MD5 and SHA-1
           compared with those taken of the original before chdman ran;
        3  the table of contents (tracks, their pauses and starts, the
           lead-out and the disc type) equal to the original disc's.

    Checks 2 and 3 are not run over a CHD that check 1 found damaged; the
    disc has failed in any case. Each converted disc is given a linked
    package in Packages/<console>/, whose package.json names the CHD and
    records the original dump: every track's hashes, the sheet's, the
    form, and the CHD's codecs and SHA-1s.

    A disc already converted, whose package names a CHD still at the size
    and time recorded and whose originals have not changed, is skipped,
    so that a stopped batch run again converts only the rest. A disc is
    started only with the free space of its original and 64 MiB more at
    the output; one whose original changes during its conversion is
    discarded; a zip of several tracks with no sheet, a sheet naming a
    WAVE file, and a file that is neither a raw track nor an ISO image are
    skipped with the reason. One disc failing never stops a batch.

    --fast writes chdman's Zstandard codecs, cdzs,cdfl, in place of its
    defaults, cdlz,cdzl,cdfl: about 1.3 to 1.8 times faster to decode for
    a CHD about a point larger. -j sets the threads chdman compresses
    with; all of the machine's by default.

    'check' runs the three checks again on discs already converted,
    against their packages; the originals are not read. 'ls' lists the
    disc games with their state: converted, not converted, changed (an
    original changed since its conversion) or missing (its CHD is gone).

JOBS
    Each command runs as a job, followed one line a disc until it ends or
    the line's budget is spent. A job that outlives the budget goes on in
    the background as chd<n>: 'ps' lists it with how far it has got,
    'chd wait chd<n>' follows it again, and 'kill chd<n>' stops it, which
    ends chdman and removes its partial file, so that nothing of the
    current disc is left. Every disc's outcome is also written, one line
    each, to Logs/chd_<day>.log.

CHDMAN
    The chdman run is the one pinned in Platform/library-tool/chdman.pin:
    MAME 0.289, built from MAME's source archive by build-chdman.sh. A
    chdman whose SHA-256 is not the pinned one is refused with both
    hashes named, and nothing is converted. chdman's source file is under
    the BSD 3-Clause License, copyright Aaron Giles, and MAME as a whole
    under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or later;
    THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md lists its licences and the written offer of
    its source.

EXAMPLES
    chd ls psx
    chd convert psx SCD
    chd convert "psx/Crash Bandicoot (USA).bin" --fast
    chd check psx
    ps
    chd wait chd1
    kill chd1

SOURCES
    MAME Documentation 0.289, "chdman - CHD (Compressed Hunks of Data)
    File Manager", https://docs.mamedev.org/tools/chdman.html: the
    commands createcd, verify and info, the codecs and createcd's
    defaults.
    MAME Documentation 0.289, "The MAME License",
    https://docs.mamedev.org/license.html.
    MAME Documentation 0.289, "Compiling MAME",
    https://docs.mamedev.org/initialsetup/compilingmame.html: TOOLS=1 and
    EMULATOR=0, by which chdman is built alone.

SEE ALSO
    EmuSen_GamePackages.md (sections 4 and 12: the design, the checks,
    the events and the measurements), EmuSen_Galaxia.md (section 3.10,
    the package manifest), EmuSen_Disc.md (how a CHD is read)
status 0
action none
end

case the page for pkg
target none
covers 1
$ man pkg
out 15483
NAME
    pkg - make game packages, linked or self-contained, list them, check
    them, zip them, copy a game's saves, states and cheats into its
    package, and give it soft patches

SYNOPSIS
    pkg make <path>... [--self-contained] [--zip]
    pkg ls [<console>]
    pkg check [<package>...]
    pkg migrate <package>...
    pkg patch add <package> <file>
    pkg patch rm <package> <n>
    pkg zip <package>...
    pkg identity <package> original|patched
    pkg wait [pkg<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The package commands of the emusen.library module. A game package is
    a folder under Packages holding package.json, its manifest, and
    everything EmuSen keeps for that game: its battery saves in saves/,
    its states and their pictures in states/, and its cheat list in
    cheats.json. A linked package, the default, names the game where it
    already is in the library, with its size, its time and the dump's
    CRC-32, MD5 and SHA-1, and nothing is copied. A self-contained package
    holds a copy of the game itself, so that the folder alone is the whole
    game (see SELF-CONTAINED PACKAGES AND ZIPS).

    THE LIBRARY IS ONLY READ. Nothing under AppSettings.RomDirectory is
    written, renamed or removed: no package, no link, no sidecar. Saves
    and states an earlier build kept in the library are copied out of it
    and left where they are. Making a package self-contained copies the
    game out of the library and never moves or removes the original.

    'make' makes a package for each game under each <path>, a file or a
    folder of the library written relative to it with / between its parts
    (nes, "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"); / is the whole library. A game
    is what the library view shows: a cue sheet and its tracks are one, as
    are an .m3u and its discs; its console is the library scan's routing,
    then the nearest console folder above it. The package goes to
    Packages/<console>/, at the game's path less the library's console
    folder, named as its file is (nes/USA/Beta.nes becomes
    Packages/NES/USA/Beta.nes/), as ES-DE names a folder it takes as one
    game. A game a package already claims is said as 'already'. Packages
    are made only when asked, never by a scan.

    With --self-contained, the game and every file it brings (a sheet's
    tracks, an .m3u's discs) are copied into the package, at the places
    the sheet or the list names them, each copy keeping its original's
    time and checked against the bytes read from it; then the manifest
    names the copies ("where": "package"), says "self-contained", and
    keeps the files they were copied from as "sources". A game already in
    a linked package has that package made self-contained ('contained'),
    its saves, states and settings staying where they are. A game a
    converted disc's package holds is copied from Compressed. A file
    already in the package of the same bytes is kept, so a stopped make
    can be run again; any other file there refuses the copy.

    With --zip (which implies --self-contained), the package is also
    written as one zip beside its folder, Packages/<console>/<name>.zip,
    as described below; a package already self-contained is only zipped
    ('zipped'). A zip already there is left as it is, and the make says
    so: move it aside to write it again.

    'zip' writes each self-contained <package>, relative to Packages, as
    one zip beside its folder, as it now is, with every save and state
    it holds: the way to carry a package on from a machine whose library
    does not hold its game, as when it came there as a zip. A linked
    package is refused, since its zip would hold no game.

    'ls' lists the packages, or one console's (by its name, its system's
    ES-DE name, or its folder under Packages), with their mode and state:
    ok; changed, the game is no longer the dump recorded; moved, the game
    is gone from its place and a file of its size and hashes is elsewhere
    in the library, which is named; missing, the game or one of its files
    is gone and not found; refused, a manifest this build does not read.
    A file is hashed only when its size or time differs from the one
    recorded, and ls writes nothing.

    'check' hashes the game and every file of each <package>, a folder
    relative to Packages (a console's folder names each package in it;
    none names them all) or a package's zip, relative to Packages or by
    its full path, against the manifest: the whole file, or for a
    cue sheet the sheet and every track; for a zip the archive and the
    game inside it. A moved game is found again and the manifest names its
    new place; a game whose time alone changed has its time recorded. A
    converted disc's package is checked by its CHD's size and time, and
    'chd check' runs its three checks. A zip is unpacked into a hidden
    folder of Packages, every entry held to the CRC-32 the zip records,
    checked there as a folder is, and the folder removed; the zip itself
    is only read. A self-contained package whose copy is gone is said
    missing: it is never linked to a library file again unasked.

    'migrate' copies the game's stores into each <package> once: from
    Saves/<console>/ (and the places earlier builds kept them: the flat
    Saves folder for the SNES and N64, beside the ROM for the NES and the
    Game Boy) every file named for the game, its battery save and its own
    save media; from the state folder (AppSettings.StateDirectory, else
    Saves/Save States) its states, its resume state and their pictures;
    its cheat list; each copied state's record from states.db, written
    beside the state in the package as the same name with .json; the
    game's own settings (its engine among them) from
    /etc/EmuSen/games/<system>/, with its crop from games.db, into the
    package's settings.json; and its art from the media store
    (home/Media/<system>/<type>/) into the package's media/<type>/ under
    the package's name. A file is the game's when it is the game's name
    followed by one or two parts of a dot and letters or digits (.srm,
    .1.mcr), so that "Super Mario Bros. 3" is never "Super Mario Bros".
    Nothing is overwritten: a file already in the package wins, a crop is
    added to a settings.json only where it holds none, and running
    migrate again copies nothing. The originals are left as they were,
    states.db and games.db among them, which are only read. The records
    and the crop need the program's SQLite; where none is loaded, migrate
    says the records were not copied, and running it again in a program
    that has one copies them. Shared and named cards, the console's own
    memory, the library's records (favourites, play time) and scraped
    metadata are not moved.

    Once a package holds them, its game's stores are read there by every
    route to the game, its plain path and its package alike, and travel
    with the package when it is moved or renamed: a state's record while
    the state is still the file it was written for, the settings and the
    crop once the package has a settings.json (until then the game's own
    file and games.db are read, and the first change copies them in), and
    the art before the media store's.

    'patch add' gives <package> a soft patch: an IPS, UPS or BPS file
    that is applied to the game each time it is loaded, so that the core
    runs the patched game while the dump in the library keeps every byte
    and hash it had. The patch is checked before anything is written:
    a UPS or BPS patch against the source checksum it carries, refused
    with both checksums named when the game is another image (or when it
    is the patched image already); an IPS patch, which carries none, is
    pinned to the SHA-1 of the image it is added to. Where a patch was
    made for the game's comparable form, the image with its copier header
    taken off, the words say so; the patch is still not added. The file
    is copied into the package's root and appended to its list, which is
    applied in order, each patch to the image the one before made; <file>
    itself is only read. The manifest records each patch's SHA-1 and
    checksums, and the hashes of the patched image as 'patched'.

    The copies are named as RetroArch finds patches beside a game when
    the list allows it: one BPS or one UPS patch as <name>.bps or
    <name>.ups, IPS patches alone as <name>.ips, <name>.ips1, <name>.ips2
    in their order, <name> being the package's own. Any other list keeps
    each file's own name, and 'ls' and 'patch' say that RetroArch will not
    apply it.

    'patch rm' takes patch <n> (from 1, in the order 'ls' and the manifest
    list them) out of the list and removes its copy from the package; the
    rest are renamed as above. It is refused, and nothing changes, when
    the patches after it would no longer apply without it.

    Disc games take no patches here: disc patches are left to a page of
    their own. A patched image may be 64 MiB at most.

    'identity' sets which identity the scraper sends for <package>:
    'original', the dump's hashes, or 'patched', the patched image's
    first and the dump's after a miss, for a hack ScreenScraper lists in
    its own right. A package with no soft patches has no patched
    identity, and 'patched' is refused for it. The identity changes
    nothing else: which saves a patched game keeps is decided by its
    patch list (below), not by what the scraper is sent.

SOFT PATCHES AT LOAD
    Opening a patched package, by its folder or by its game's plain path,
    applies its list in both runtimes before the core is made: every file
    is held to the SHA-1 recorded, every checksum is checked, and the
    result is held to the 'patched' hashes. Anything that fails refuses
    the game with words naming the patch and both values; the core is
    never given a half-patched image, and the game's file is never
    written. 'check' does the same without a core: the game, then every
    patch and what they make. A state saved on a patched game records the
    patched image's MD5, so that a state of another list is asked about.
    The scraper sends the dump's hashes for a patched package unless its
    manifest's "identity" is "patched", when it sends the patched image's
    first and the dump's after a miss.

    A patched game keeps its own battery save, save media and states,
    apart from the original's: in the package's saves and states folders
    as <name>.patched-<key>, <key> being eight hexadecimal digits of the
    hash of the list, each patch's format and SHA-1 in order. Each list
    reaches its own saves, the same list always the same ones, and with
    every patch removed the game returns to the original's. A patched
    game starts its save empty: the original's is never read or written
    by it.

SELF-CONTAINED PACKAGES AND ZIPS
    A self-contained package needs nothing outside its folder: its game,
    saves, states and their records, settings, art, cheats and patches
    are all in it. Copied whole to another machine's Packages folder,
    with the files' times kept (cp -p, rsync -a, or the package's zip),
    it opens there as it did here. A copy that loses the times loses the
    states' records, which are stamped with their states' times, and
    never the states, which load as any state without a record does; the
    game is hashed once at its next opening and its time recorded.

    A package's zip holds every file of its folder under one folder named
    as the package, so that any unzip gives back the package's folder.
    A CHD and what is already compressed (pictures, video, zips) are
    stored as they are, never compressed again; everything else is
    deflated, or stored where deflating would not make it smaller. Each
    file keeps its time to a ten-millionth of a second (the NTFS extra
    field), as well as to the second (the extended timestamp) and in the
    DOS fields. A zip holds at most 65,534 files and 4 GiB, as no Zip64 is
    written.

    EmuSen opens a package's zip as the package: the first time it is
    opened, from the library, 'core' or a recent list, it is unpacked into
    Packages, beside itself when it lies there and straight under
    Packages when it lies anywhere else, every entry checked and given
    its time, and the package's folder opens. After that the folder is
    the package and the zip is only read: a zip whose folder is there
    opens the folder, and a zip is never unpacked over a folder, so a zip
    brought back from another machine does not replace the saves of a
    folder that is still here. The library lists a zip beside its own
    folder once, as the folder.

    Opening the library file a self-contained package was copied from
    opens the package's copy with the package's saves, so that the game
    has one save, as a linked package's game does; the library lists the
    game once, as the package's. This holds while the library file is
    still the one it was copied from (its size and time, else its SHA-1,
    as recorded in "sources"); changed, it opens as a plain game with its
    plain saves, as a changed claimed file does.

ONE GAME, ONE SAVE
    Once a package claims a game, opening the game by its plain path, from
    'core', the library or a recent list, opens it as the package: its
    battery save and states are the package's, and a save kept in Saves
    before is read until the first write copies it in. A package claims
    its game, its companions (a sheet's tracks, an .m3u's discs) and the
    library files a converted disc was made from. A claimed game whose
    size or time changed is hashed when it is opened: still the dump, its
    time is recorded; another dump, the package is changed. Opened by its
    plain path such a file plays as a plain game with its plain saves,
    and the package's are left as they were; the package opened itself
    is refused with both hashes named, and 'ls' and 'check' say changed.
    A game that moved in the library is found again by its size and then
    its hashes when it or its package is opened, and the manifest is
    rewritten to name where it now is.

JOBS
    Each command runs as a job, followed one line a package until it ends
    or the line's budget is spent; a longer one goes on in the background
    as pkg<n>: 'ps' lists it, 'pkg wait pkg<n>' follows it again, and
    'kill pkg<n>' stops it, the packages not reached left as they are.
    make, check and migrate write each package's outcome, one line each,
    to Logs/pkg_<day>.log.

EXAMPLES
    pkg make nes
    pkg make "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg make --self-contained --zip "snes/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg check "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc.zip"
    pkg zip "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg ls NES
    pkg check NES
    pkg migrate "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc"
    pkg patch add "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" ~/Patches/Redesign.bps
    pkg patch rm "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" 1
    pkg identity "SNES/Super Metroid (USA).sfc" patched
    pkg wait pkg1

SEE ALSO
    chd (converted discs, each given a package), EmuSen_GamePackages.md
    (sections 3, 5.4, 5.5, 6, 14, 15 and 19: the package, its manifest,
    the stores it takes, soft patches, other frontends, and what each step
    built), EmuSen_Galaxia.md (sections 3.10, 3.11 and 3.13: the
    manifest, the claim index and a package's zip)
status 0
action none
end
